(function(){'use strict';var lib=window.JASPER_FANFIC_DATA;if(!lib||!Array.isArray(lib.series))return;var edits={"maui-14":{"number":14,"title":"The Quiet Work of Rope","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — expansion pass three","content":"The rope snapped with a sound like somebody cracking a whip across the shore.\n\nI saw Maui's hand jerk backward. The mooring post shuddered. A boat that had been safely tucked against the Tideglass landing swung toward the rocks, and the two children sitting inside it screamed.\n\n\"Maui!\"\n\nHe was already moving.\n\nHe caught the bow with his hook before the boat could strike the stone. His feet planted in the wet sand, knees bent, and for a moment the whole dock seemed to strain against him. Then Fen reached the line, dragged it through the remaining ring, and tied off the boat while I helped the children onto the steps.\n\nThey were frightened. They were unhurt.\n\nThe older child tried to climb back onto the dock after Fen had tied off the boat. Their shoe slipped on wet wood and Maui made a movement to catch them, but I was already holding the rail.\n\n“Wait,” Fen said sharply. “The board beside the post is loose.”\n\nMaui froze in the middle of reaching. A single plank bowed beneath the child's foot. Tamsin appeared with a wooden brace and asked both children to sit on the dry step while she checked the fixings.\n\n“I could lift the whole dock,” Maui offered.\n\n“You could,” she said. “And then I'd have to explain to every fisher in Tideglass why the landing was gone.”\n\nMini Maui on his shoulder acted out lifting a miniature pier and being crushed by an imaginary fish. The younger child giggled. I saw Maui's expression change when he realized they were laughing, not screaming.\n\nTamsin found a snapped bolt, which she put in a cloth pouch. She asked the children what had happened before the mooring line went. The younger one said they'd heard the rope scraping; the older remembered seeing a frayed patch that morning but hadn't known whom to tell.\n\nFen crouched at the step. “You can tell any dock worker. Or me. You won't get anyone in trouble for pointing out a bad rope.”\n\nMaui rubbed his cut hand against his side.\n\n“I saw the rope,” he said after a moment. “I thought I could handle it.”\n\nTamsin looked at him. “You did handle the boat. The dock is still our job to make safe.”\n\nHe looked almost annoyed by the distinction, then nodded.\n\nOnce the children had gone home with a neighbor, I found Maui studying the bolt in the pouch.\n\n“What?” I asked.\n\n“I've fought monsters bigger than this pier.” He held up two fingers. “And a piece of iron this small nearly put two children in the water.”\n\nHe wasn't looking for comfort. He was angry at the gap between what he could lift and what he had failed to notice. I stood close enough for our shoulders to touch and let that anger be real.\n\nMaui was still smiling when I turned toward him.\n\n\"See?\" he announced, a little too loudly. \"Boat rescued. Children rescued. Hero—still devastatingly handsome.\" He gave the children a wink, then flexed his bleeding hand behind his back as though he could hide the pain from the entire shoreline.\n\nHis left hand was bleeding.\n\n\"Show me.\"\n\n\"It's a scratch.\"\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\nHe held it up. There was a raw stripe across the base of his palm, deeper at one end where the rope had caught. He flexed his fingers and tried to hide the wince under a grin.\n\n\"Hero's scratch.\"\n\n\"Well, the hero is going to sit down.\"\n\nFen looked at the broken line and swore softly.\n\n\"That shouldn't have given way.\"\n\nI checked the end. One part was frayed, but a second section looked almost polished. The strand had been rubbing against the underside of the landing, where a nail had shifted during the recent repairs.\n\n\"Maintenance failure,\" I said.\n\nFen nodded.\n\n\"Not a monster?\"\n\nMaui looked mildly offended.\n\n\"I could still fight one.\"\n\n\"Try fighting a clean bandage first.\"\n\nThe clinic didn't open until noon, but Katara had left an emergency kit in the public storage cabinet. I made Maui sit on an overturned crate while I cleaned the cut. He watched with an expression of tremendous patience that lasted perhaps thirty seconds.\n\n\"I could heal faster as a hawk.\"\n\n\"Do hawks have hands?\"\n\n\"Not technically.\"\n\n\"Then stay as you are.\"\n\nHe examined the wound over my shoulder.\n\n\"I've had worse.\"\n\n\"I believe you.\"\n\n\"Much worse.\"\n\n\"Still believe you.\"\n\n\"That time with the eel—\"\n\n\"If you tell me this cut is nothing compared to an eel, I am going to make the bandage extremely decorative.\"\n\nHe shut his mouth.\n\nFen took the broken line to Tamsin's workshop. The children went home with Mara, who offered bread and a stern lecture about staying seated while docking. By the time I finished the bandage, the tide had begun to turn.\n\nMaui reached for his hook.\n\n\"No,\" I said.\n\nHe stared at me.\n\n\"No?\"\n\n\"Not until we know whether that hand needs proper treatment. And not until somebody else examines the rest of the moorings.\"\n\n\"I can inspect them.\"\n\n\"I know you can. That's not the problem.\"\n\n\"Then what is?\"\n\nI wanted to say that he was exhausting. I wanted to say that I couldn't bear the thought of him being hurt again, because the sight of blood on his hand had made something awful open inside my chest.\n\nNeither would explain why the dock had failed.\n\n\"The problem,\" I said carefully, \"is that everyone assumes you're the solution to every emergency. Even you.\"\n\nFor a long moment he said nothing.\n\nThen he looked toward the children disappearing into the square.\n\n\"They were scared.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"And I could help.\"\n\n\"You did help. Now other people get to help too.\"\n\nHe looked down at the bandage.\n\n\"Doesn't feel right.\"\n\nThat was the most honest thing he'd said all morning.\n\nAt Tamsin's workshop, Fen showed us three knots with different jobs. One held under steady weight, one could be untied after a sudden pull, and the third was designed so a damaged line could be replaced without anyone putting a hand where the load would crush it.\n\nI got the first one wrong twice.\n\nMaui got it right immediately, then tried to demonstrate with his injured hand.\n\nMini Maui appeared at his wrist and performed an entire play about a heroic injured hand. It ended with the miniature collapsing dramatically beside a rope.\n\n\"I know,\" Maui said. \"I'm suffering.\"\n\n\"You're showing off,\" I said.\n\n\"Two things can be true.\"\n\nFen pulled the line toward them and pointed to the section that would carry the actual load. Maui leaned closer, interested despite himself. He'd tied enough lines in his life to make the motions look effortless, but he was watching how Fen tested this particular wet rope.\n\n\"Is this how all of you pass the time?\" he asked. \"Inventing ways to make a demigod feel inadequate?\"\n\n\"Mostly we repair the damage caused by people who never check their knots.\"\n\nMaui looked wounded. Then he grinned and offered his uninjured hand.\n\n\"Fine. Tell me what the legendary Maui has to do to pass your tiny rope exam.\"\n\nHe listened to the answer. His hand was shaking by the end, partly from the cut and partly from how determined he was not to be the person who made the next boat unsafe. The grin stayed. So did the effort.\n\nFen slapped the rope away.\n\n\"Use the other one.\"\n\n\"You're very demanding for a person teaching knots.\"\n\n\"You're very loud for somebody who hasn't paid the repair bill.\"\n\nMaui blinked.\n\n\"There's a bill?\"\n\n\"There is always a bill.\"\n\nFen pointed to the ledger on the wall. Rope cost money. Nails cost money. Tamsin's time cost money. So did replacement planks. The Lanternweald's dock fees paid for some things, but not everything, and the people who kept boats floating weren't decorative figures in somebody else's epic.\n\nWe all looked at the ledger.\n\nTwo weeks of repairs had nearly emptied the landing fund.\n\n\"Why didn't you tell us?\" I asked.\n\n\"Because half the village offers to help for free and the other half feels guilty about not being able to,\" Fen said. \"I need the tools paid for. I don't need a parade.\"\n\nMaui was quiet.\n\nI wondered whether he was considering selling a legend to cover the price of nails.\n\nInstead he asked, \"How much for the rope?\"\n\nFen named an amount.\n\n\"I can pay some,\" Maui said.\n\n\"You don't owe the whole dock because you were here when it failed.\"\n\n\"I know. But I use it.\"\n\nFen looked surprised.\n\n\"That's different.\"\n\nI spent the afternoon helping inspect the mooring points. We marked each one with colored thread, not to rank them but to show which were safe, which needed maintenance, and which were closed until tested. Pip offered to translate the signs for travelers who couldn't read the Lanternweald script. Toph arrived and tapped the planks with her heel.\n\n\"Wood's talking,\" she announced.\n\n\"Does it have good news?\" I asked.\n\n\"Some of it's begging to be replaced.\"\n\nShe marked two supports we'd missed.\n\nMaui hated staying on the bench. He was especially offended when a small child offered him a cup of water and called him brave for sitting still.\n\n\"That was sarcastic,\" he said after the child left.\n\n\"That was generous.\"\n\n\"You're enjoying this.\"\n\n\"Immensely.\"\n\nBy sunset the dock was closed, the replacement list was posted, and nobody had suffered another injury.\n\nI found Maui alone at the end of the cove. He held a short length of spare rope in his good hand, turning it over as if it were a puzzle.\n\n\"Fen taught me something,\" he said.\n\n\"A knot?\"\n\n\"No. Well, yes. But something else.\" He pulled one loop through another. \"When I was younger, people always wanted proof I was strong. Then when I became strong, they wanted proof I could do anything. I got used to being the person nobody had to ask.\"\n\nI sat beside him.\n\n\"Does it frighten you to be asked to stop?\"\n\nHe took a long time to answer.\n\n\"It frightens me that if I stop, you might discover you never needed me.\"\n\nThe words made me ache.\n\n\"Maui.\"\n\nHe looked at me with an openness so unlike his stage voice that I barely recognized it.\n\n\"I need you,\" I said. \"But not because you can hold a boat against the sea. I need the person who remembered I hate my tea too sweet. The one who painted a crab on a safety sign and then made sure it was the correct kind of crab.\"\n\n\"It is a very accurate crab.\"\n\n\"The most accurate.\"\n\nHe laughed once.\n\n\"I need you to tell me when you're scared,\" I continued. \"And I need you to let somebody else hold a rope when you're hurt.\"\n\nHe looked at his bandage.\n\n\"Even if they're bad at knots?\"\n\n\"Especially then. They can learn.\"\n\nHe looped his good arm around my shoulders. I leaned against him. His cheek brushed my hair, warm from the sun, and the smell of salt and timber rose between us.\n\n\"I could teach you the next knot,\" he murmured.\n\n\"Tomorrow. Tonight you're coming home.\"\n\n\"Bossy.\"\n\n\"You like it.\"\n\n\"Terribly.\"\n\nHe kissed me, slow enough that I could feel his smile become something gentler. We went home before the tide rose.\n\nThe following morning, the broken mooring was still broken. The ledger still needed funds. But Fen had drawn a new maintenance rota, Tamsin had priced the supplies, and Maui had signed up for a shift he could do with one hand.\n\nNobody owed him applause.\n\nHe seemed relieved.","word_count":1931},"maui-17":{"number":17,"title":"A Lantern for the Absent","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fourth expansion pass","content":"I couldn't decide what name to write on the lantern.\n\nThat was the trouble. There were names I remembered, names I had learned again, and names that lived just beyond my reach like objects at the bottom of dark water. If I wrote one, the others felt abandoned. If I wrote them all, I would have to admit how many empty spaces remained.\n\nMara watched me stare at the paper.\n\n\"You're allowed to leave it blank,\" she said.\n\n\"Everyone else is writing something.\"\n\n\"Everyone else is doing what they want.\"\n\nShe dipped her brush into a little pot of amber paint and continued a pattern of waves around her lantern. She had written nothing on it either.\n\n\"Who is yours for?\" I asked.\n\n\"Somebody who hated being named in public.\"\n\nI looked at her.\n\n\"Then why bring it?\"\n\n\"Because I still want to carry a light.\"\n\nThat answer stayed with me.\n\nFrostturn wasn't quite winter in Lanternweald. The weather didn't obey any one homeworld's calendar. The name belonged to a local stretch of short days, when the first hard frost reached the canal reeds and the western wind brought the smell of cold seaweed into Lanternwell Square. The Lantern Walk at Frostturn had once been a way for travelers to find their doors before night overtook the paths. Nowadays the district stewards still checked the crossings, but people also carried lights for memory, gratitude, mourning, or no reason at all.\n\nPip had painted signs showing the accessible route. Edda and Tamsin had marked the stretches where the cobbles were uneven. Danse had tested the pier lamps, and Iroh had insisted on hot tea for anyone who needed to stop along the way.\n\nNobody had to dedicate a lantern to anyone.\n\nI knew this. I had helped write the notice.\n\nKnowing wasn't the same as believing it for myself.\n\nMaui swept toward the table carrying a basket of spare wicks and wearing a cloak with a fur collar so dramatic it could have belonged to a minor king. Someone had apparently told him the vigil wasn't a fashion event; he had treated that as a challenge. His grin eased when he saw my untouched lantern.\n\n\"I bring supplies,\" he announced, lifting the wicker basket like a trophy. \"For what I assume is an extremely sophisticated lantern-making ceremony.\"\n\n\"It's paper and string.\"\n\n\"And bravery. Don't undersell bravery.\"\n\nMini Maui had somehow acquired an imaginary fur collar of his own. He strutted across the larger tattooed chest until Maui pressed a finger against the tiny figure and told it to behave.\n\nI smiled despite the knot in my throat.\n\nHis expression softened a fraction. \"Hey. I don't know who you're writing to. I don't have to.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"But if you'd like company, my company is famously exceptional.\"\n\n\"And if I want to sit here without anyone talking?\"\n\nHe looked aghast. \"An unfair test of my greatest weakness.\"\n\nI laughed. He sat anyway, made a show of sealing his lips, and lasted perhaps forty seconds before whispering that he'd defeated a storage cabinet that morning. Then he caught himself, shrugged, and pushed the basket toward me. I didn't need him to become silent or saintly. I needed him to recognize that the unnamed people I mourned were real, even when he couldn't put himself in their story.\n\n\"How many enemies have you defeated?\" I asked.\n\n\"Today? None.\"\n\n\"Tragic.\"\n\n\"I did battle with a stubborn storage cabinet.\"\n\n\"Did you win?\"\n\n\"It surrendered two hinges and a spider.\"\n\nHe put the basket down and looked at my blank lantern.\n\n\"Too many names?\"\n\nI nodded.\n\nHe didn't ask which ones.\n\nHe sat beside me and threaded a wick through a little brass ring. His injured hand had healed enough for ordinary work, though Fen still wouldn't let him touch the heaviest mooring ropes.\n\nAfter a while he said, \"I used to tell stories where everyone remembered me.\"\n\n\"That's not a surprise.\"\n\n\"No. But I thought if I made the story big enough, nobody would forget the people I'd lost along the way. I could keep them with me by talking.\"\n\n\"Did it work?\"\n\n\"For a while. Then I realized some people wanted to be remembered quietly.\"\n\nI ran my thumb over the paper.\n\n\"Some of mine might not even know I'm here.\"\n\n\"Do you want to go looking for them?\"\n\n\"Someday. Maybe. Not because tonight tells me I have to.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\nI picked up the brush and painted a small circle in the center of the lantern. I left a gap at the top, an opening too small for a name but large enough for light.\n\n\"That's it?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"For now.\"\n\n\"It's beautiful.\"\n\n\"You haven't seen it lit.\"\n\n\"I know what I like.\"\n\nI smiled, then caught myself worrying that I had been unfair to him. He had listened to my grief so patiently, and I wasn't sure how to explain that loving him did not close the spaces I'd carried from before.\n\n\"Maui?\"\n\n\"Hm?\"\n\n\"Can I ask you something awkward?\"\n\n\"I thrive on awkward questions.\"\n\n\"Do you?\"\n\n\"Absolutely not. Ask anyway.\"\n\nI put down the brush.\n\n\"You know I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory. I've told you that from the beginning, and I haven't changed my mind.\"\n\nHis expression remained open.\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Sometimes I worry that when I miss people, you'll think I'm wishing you were someone else.\"\n\nHe looked at the lantern.\n\n\"I do wonder whether I'll be enough for the stories you haven't remembered.\"\n\n\"That's not a fair job to give you.\"\n\n\"I know.\" He hesitated. \"But being a hero makes it very tempting to think every problem needs me.\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"You don't need to replace anyone. You don't need to be the only person I love. I want you here because you're Maui, and because I choose you.\"\n\nHe let out a breath.\n\n\"And if you choose another person too?\"\n\n\"Then I tell the truth. I don't expect you to agree to something just because it makes sense to me. We talk about boundaries, and you get to decide what you want.\"\n\nHe was quiet for a moment.\n\n\"I want to be someone you can come home to,\" he said. \"Without making home a cage.\"\n\nI reached for his hand.\n\n\"That's what I want too.\"\n\nWe stayed at the table until the lanterns were ready. Then Mara called for help, and the square became a pleasant disaster of people trying to carry hot drinks, spare wicks, and weatherproof coats at the same time.\n\nIroh arrived with a kettle. Greg had brought a small hand drum so people could keep pace without turning the walk into a concert. Toph tested the route by feeling the ground beneath her boots, and Aang helped the children hang lanterns where they could reach them safely.\n\nMaui carried my lantern.\n\nHe didn't ask what it meant.\n\nThe procession began at Lanternwell. The bells rang once, soft and ordinary. Along the canal, the lights stretched into a line that seemed to float just above the water. Frost had caught in the reeds, making them shine at their edges.\n\nI walked beside Maui past Jasmine Steps, then toward the cove.\n\nA woman ahead of us stopped at a bench. She held a lantern painted with three small stars. Her face was wet. Nobody asked her to explain. Edda moved a cup of tea within reach, and the woman nodded her thanks.\n\nIt occurred to me that privacy could be a kind of hospitality. Not silence forced on someone who needed to speak, but room for the choice.\n\nAt the water, Maui lifted my lantern toward a hook beneath the signal post.\n\n\"Here?\"\n\n\"Here.\"\n\nHe hung it carefully.\n\nThe flame caught the circle I'd painted, spilling light through the little gap.\n\nFor one terrible, beautiful moment I wanted to tell him every name. I wanted to invent the names I couldn't remember, if only to fill the absence.\n\nInstead, I leaned against him and whispered, \"I miss them.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nHe didn't ask who.\n\nI turned my face into his shoulder. His hand settled at the back of my coat, warm and steady.\n\n\"May I kiss you?\" he asked quietly.\n\nI nodded.\n\nHis lips met mine beneath the lantern, gentle enough that grief could remain in the room without being crowded out. I kissed him again because I wanted to, not because it made the night less sad.\n\nLater we walked home after most of the lights had been extinguished. There were still several hanging along the safe route, each marked with a steward's name so no one would assume somebody else had put them out.\n\nAt our door, Maui paused.\n\n\"Do you want company?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And quiet?\"\n\n\"Mostly.\"\n\n\"I can do mostly.\"\n\nFen came down the shore steps carrying the lantern tally, wick scissors, and a bent metal bracket.\n\n\"The west hook comes out of service until Tamsin checks the beam,\" Fen said.\n\nMaui looked over. \"I could fix that.\"\n\n\"You could hold the ladder while I remove it.\"\n\nHis expression suggested somebody had refused to applaud the sunrise. Mini Maui acted out a terrible fall from a miniature ladder, then crossed his arms in a tiny performance of outrage.\n\n\"You're supposed to be on my side,\" Maui told his tattoo.\n\nFen handed him the ladder. He planted its feet in the sand and kept them steady while Fen loosened the bracket. He didn't offer to pull the beam apart, replace the whole pier, or demonstrate how strong he was. I knew how much effort that took.\n\n\"The lantern list doesn't require names,\" Fen told me. \"I only need to know which hooks are safe. Nobody checking the tally gets to decide whom you're missing.\"\n\nWhen Fen left, Maui flexed his hands and gave me an injured look.\n\n\"Not one monster. Not a single heroic rescue. I was magnificent at standing still, and there's no audience.\"\n\n\"I was watching.\"\n\n\"Ah. My favorite audience.\"\n\nThe humor slipped from his face when he saw the blank space on my lantern. He didn't ask me to fill it.\n\n\"Do you want me here?\" he asked.\n\nI took his hand. \"Yes. Not because you can hold a ladder.\"\n\n\"Good, because that ladder was vicious. Tried to stand upright the whole time.\"\n\nI kissed him, and for a moment he seemed surprised enough to forget his next boast. The lantern remained unmarked, the bracket stayed off the rail, and the tide went on moving without asking us to explain our grief.\n\nI laughed, tired and grateful. We sat with a blanket and a small bowl of warmed bread until the fire settled into coals.\n\nThe lantern at Tideglass burned until dawn.\n\nIt held no names.\n\nIt did not forget anyone.","word_count":1791},"maui-32":{"number":32,"title":"The Shape of an Agreement","subtitle":"Lanternweald — sixth expansion pass","content":"The calendar had survived salt spray, one dropped kettle, and three months of Maui writing appointments in letters large enough to be seen from the harbor. It did not survive his attempt to improve it.\n\nI found it on the kitchen table with a line drawn through Thursday.\n\nThursday had been ours.\n\nHe had replaced it with a note: *Helping Fen. Necessary.*\n\nI stood there holding a parcel from Moonroot Archive, still wearing the coat I'd put on that morning to come home early. We'd planned the evening two weeks ago. Not a festival, not a rescue, not a shift at the pantry. Dinner, the little blue blanket, and absolutely no public meetings.\n\n\"Maui?\"\n\nHe came in carrying a coil of rope. \"Before you say anything, the new mooring—\"\n\n\"You canceled our evening.\"\n\nHe looked at the calendar. \"Moved it! Brand-new day. It's like a heroic rescue, except for a dinner plan. Friday's open.\"\n\n\"Friday Iroh and I are reviewing the terrace map.\"\n\n\"Then Saturday.\"\n\n\"Saturday you promised Lio you'd inspect the skiff.\"\n\nHis smile faltered. \"Right.\"\n\nI set the parcel beside the stove. The air smelled like wet rope and rosemary. I wanted to say something spectacularly unfair, something about being last on his list. A familiar, uglier part of me wanted to win the argument before he knew it had begun.\n\nInstead I said, \"You didn't ask me.\"\n\nMaui put down the rope. \"Fen needed someone to help secure the landing before the wind shifts.\"\n\n\"Fen could need help. I might even agree that it's urgent. Neither changes the fact that you decided what happened to my evening without telling me.\"\n\n\"I thought you'd understand.\"\n\n\"I do understand the mooring. I don't understand why you didn't send a note.\"\n\nHe rubbed his face. \"I was busy.\"\n\n\"So busy you had time to rewrite the calendar.\"\n\nThat landed.\n\nHe looked at the enormous letters he'd written across Thursday. \"Okay. That was bad.\"\n\nI pulled out a chair. \"Sit with me?\"\n\nHe sat, awkwardly. His injured hand had healed, but he still curled his fingers when he was ashamed, as if bracing for a blow that wasn't coming.\n\n\"I made a mistake,\" he said. \"I should have asked.\"\n\n\"Thank you.\"\n\n\"I need to know something, too.\"\n\nI waited.\n\n\"Sometimes I look at that calendar and feel like I'm asking for an appointment with someone who has six lives. Then I hate myself for thinking it, because you told me from the beginning that you're ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. I agreed to that knowing what it meant.\"\n\n\"I don't have six lives,\" I said. \"I have one. It's the same me coming to your door.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Knowing doesn't mean you stop feeling lonely.\"\n\nHe looked at me, surprised.\n\nI turned the calendar around. Its edges were curled. Under my appointments he'd added tiny drawings of fish and hooks. Beneath my private time with Severus, he'd written *No questions*. He had been trying, in his own untidy way, to respect agreements he didn't always find easy.\n\n\"Can we write down what we actually mean?\" I asked.\n\n\"Without making it a contract that gets me arrested?\"\n\n\"Preferably.\"\n\nWe took a clean sheet from the green notebook. Across the top I wrote *Our present agreements—not ownership, not forever.*\n\nMaui leaned over to read it.\n\n\"That's a long title.\"\n\n\"We can edit.\"\n\nWe agreed to tell each other about schedule changes before altering shared plans, unless there was an immediate emergency. In that case we would send word as soon as safely possible. We agreed not to share details about my other partners' private lives without their permission. He could ask for reassurance, and I could answer honestly without ranking anybody.\n\nMost importantly, either of us could say no to a date, a conversation, a kiss, or a request for time. No explanation was required to make a no count.\n\nMaui studied the last line.\n\n\"If you say no because you need quiet, can I ask when we might talk again?\"\n\n\"Yes. That's a request, not a demand. Sometimes I won't know.\"\n\nHe nodded.\n\n\"And if I need quiet?\"\n\n\"Then you get it too.\"\n\nWe tried the new agreement on a small problem before declaring it useful.\n\n\"Suppose Fen needs you during our next dinner,\" I said.\n\nMaui looked at the list. \"I ask whether it's an emergency.\"\n\n\"And if it is?\"\n\n\"I tell you where I'm going if there's time. I send word when I can. I don't decide what you'll do with the evening.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\n\"And if it isn't?\"\n\n\"You can still choose to help, but you ask whether we can move our date before you strike it through.\"\n\nHe made a face. \"That means you might say no.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Terrible system.\"\n\nI poked him with the pencil. \"Try again.\"\n\nHe laughed. \"That means I need to decide what promise I'm keeping instead of assuming you'll wait.\"\n\nThe distinction mattered more than I expected. He could care about Fen, and he could care about me. Nobody had to become the villain because two good things asked for the same hour.\n\nI had told him I was ethically non-monogamous and preferred polyamory, but a relationship agreement wasn't just about who we kissed. It was about whether he could need reassurance without demanding my freedom, and whether I could choose to be with him without pretending all my other commitments had vanished.\n\n\"Would you like me to come with you to check the mooring tomorrow?\" I asked.\n\n\"Yes. If you want to.\"\n\n\"I can do the morning. In the afternoon I'm at Moonroot.\"\n\nHe wrote both parts down. His handwriting was so large that the last word disappeared into the margin.\n\n\"There's no room,\" he complained.\n\n\"Then we'll need another page.\"\n\nHe looked at the green notebook and reached for a blank sheet. \"Are you sure we can change this later?\"\n\n\"We have to be able to. Otherwise it becomes another door we're afraid to open.\"\n\nHe nodded, and when I leaned close he waited for me to kiss him. The warmth of his hand at my waist felt like an invitation I was still free to accept tomorrow or refuse next week. That made it sweeter, not smaller.\n\nWe left space at the bottom to revise the page. We signed it with our names, not as a legal instrument but because the act made us both laugh. Maui drew a small fish beside his signature. Its expression was deeply suspicious.\n\nThe fish beside his name looked as though it were preparing to attack the calendar.\n\n\"That's you,\" I said.\n\nMaui drew himself up. \"That is a fearsome guardian of the Thursday dinner.\"\n\n\"It's a fish with a grudge.\"\n\n\"Exactly. Deadly.\"\n\nMini Maui poked his head out of the tattoos and began miming the fish's angry expression. The real Maui looked down and pointed a warning finger.\n\n\"No sabotage. We are handling this like adults.\"\n\n\"You sound disappointed.\"\n\n\"I am extremely disappointed. Handling things like adults is exhausting. Do you know how much easier it is to fight a monster?\"\n\nI laughed. He grinned with genuine relief, then tapped the crossed-out Thursday and the newly marked Sunday.\n\n\"Still want that dinner with me?\" he asked, quieter.\n\n\"Yes. On Sunday.\"\n\n\"Good. I'm going to make something so magnificent you'll write songs about it.\"\n\n\"I'll settle for edible.\"\n\nHis offended gasp echoed into the kitchen. He left for Fen's shift on time, but not before drawing a tiny crown on the fish.\n\nI glanced at the clock.\n\n\"You still have the mooring shift?\"\n\n\"Yes. Fen's expecting me.\"\n\n\"Then go. I don't want the landing to fail because we were arguing.\"\n\nHe paused at the door. \"Are we all right?\"\n\n\"We're working on it.\"\n\nHe seemed to take that more seriously than any grand assurance I could have given.\n\nThe next morning he sent a message before the agreed hour. Fen's inspection had uncovered a worn ring. They would need another half-day, and he wanted to know whether I minded moving our meal to Sunday.\n\nI smiled at the message.\n\n*Sunday works. Ask Lio whether Saturday still suits them.*\n\nHe answered with a picture of a fish whose fins were holding a calendar.\n\nOn Sunday, we made dinner together. Maui burned the first flatbread. I misread the spice jar and filled the kitchen with cinnamon. We ate the second batch outside by the sheltered garden, where the sea kale was growing slowly but stubbornly.\n\nA messenger stopped at the outer gate before we'd finished the meal. Maui's head turned at the sound of footsteps, and I saw the old reflex take hold: somebody from the landing, a problem to fix, an excuse to be needed before the dishes had cooled.\n\nThe messenger was Pip, carrying a small square of paper and wearing a look that could have curdled milk.\n\n“Before you make that face,” they told him, “nobody is drowning. Fen wanted you to know the evening shift changed. Tomorrow's still yours, with the new rope inspection.”\n\nMaui looked almost offended. “I wasn't making a face.”\n\n“Your tattoos were making it for you.”\n\nMini Maui had already drawn a heroic hook in the air and started waving it at imaginary waves. The tiny figure froze when it noticed us staring.\n\nPip handed him the note. “Fen said to read it, not run to the dock.”\n\nMaui read it. I watched his shoulders loosen as the words sank in.\n\n“I can bring the repair ledger in the morning,” he said.\n\n“Great. Then I'll go home.” Pip turned to me. “Sorry to interrupt your dinner.”\n\n“It's okay.”\n\n“No,” Maui said, surprising both of us. “It wasn't urgent. Next time leave the note in the message box. We'll read it after the meal.”\n\nPip gave him a long look, then grinned. “Look at that. He can be taught.”\n\nWhen they'd gone, Maui stared at the paper and laughed under his breath. “I'm going to be unbearable about how well I handled that.”\n\n“Going to be?”\n\nHe pulled the blue blanket over both our knees. His thigh settled against mine; his fingers, still rough from the rope, brushed my wrist. The touch was not an apology for Thursday. It was the sort of wanting that made me stop thinking about calendars for a moment.\n\n“There's a whole night left,” he said, voice lower than before.\n\n“Then don't spend it making a speech.”\n\nHis grin flashed. “I was about to make an excellent one.”\n\n“I know.”\n\nHe folded the note and put it facedown on the table.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" he asked when we'd cleared the plates.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHis thumb brushed my cheek. He kissed me as though he didn't want the moment to finish, then drew back to ask whether I was cold.\n\n\"A little,\" I admitted.\n\nHe fetched the blue blanket.\n\nWe sat close, the sea breathing beyond the stones, and I let my head rest against him. Later we closed our door and kept the evening to ourselves.\n\nIn the morning the calendar was still hanging. Thursday was crossed out, Sunday carried a small blue star, and our new agreement lay beside the green notebook, ready to be revised when life made more honesty necessary.\n\nIt was paper.\n\nWe were the people who had to keep choosing what it meant.","word_count":1883},"maui-42":{"number":42,"title":"Where the Water Cannot Go","subtitle":"Lanternweald — sixth expansion pass","content":"Fen spread three maps on the table and weighted them down with stones.\n\nThe oldest showed a blue line running beneath the square toward Tideglass. The second, drawn after the Closed Bells, ended that line in thick black ink. The third was ours, covered in flood marks, inspection dates, and the small angry circles Tamsin drew wherever a repair still needed money.\n\n\"Water doesn't disappear because somebody closes a door,\" Fen said.\n\nToph tapped the table.\n\n\"Neither do the consequences.\"\n\nI stood beside Maui, watching Fen trace the old route.\n\nBefore the sealing, the passage had carried excess rainwater away from the terraces and floodplain into a controlled coastal outlet during certain tide conditions. It wasn't a miracle. It had gates, inspections, and periods when it couldn't be used safely. After the lower barrier was sealed, the valley had relied on smaller surface drains and a series of improvised diversions.\n\nThe diversions worked in ordinary weather.\n\nIn storms, they pushed water toward the same homes and gardens again and again.\n\nSela pointed to the map.\n\n\"Mine.\"\n\nThen another point.\n\nSela put two receipts beside the map: seedlings lost in the flood and repairs to the cart that carried them to market. Both had already been paid from her own savings.\n\n\"I don't need another lecture about how water finds a path,\" she said. \"I want to know why it keeps finding my garden.\"\n\nMaui leaned over the map, staring at the heaviest bank. \"I could move that before sunset.\"\n\n\"And flood the pottery kiln,\" Fen said. \"That's why we measure things before lifting them.\"\n\nToph put her heel against the floor. \"The ground tilts east. You shove the bank and somebody else gets your problem.\"\n\nMini Maui pressed both hands to his eyes. I touched his wrist, not to silence him, but to ask him to stay while other people finished speaking.\n\nTamsin marked three tests: an inspection of the upper surface-channel gate, a temporary footpath above the wet ground, and a fresh measurement at the next predicted high tide. The lower Closed Bells passage remained sealed. None of us had evidence that made reopening it safe.\n\nSela read the plan twice. \"And the market gets the results in writing. No festival advertisements before we know people can actually use the route.\"\n\n\"Agreed,\" I said.\n\nMaui picked up her receipts. \"Can I at least carry these to the funding desk?\"\n\n\"Only if they give you a signature,\" Sela said.\n\nMaui straightened to his full height. \"You're speaking to the greatest courier in the history of—\"\n\n\"Maui,\" I said.\n\n\"Right. Two receipts. Two signatures. Not even a tiny musical number.\"\n\nHe got both receipts signed. When he brought the copies back, Sela checked them before thanking him.\n\nIt was a small victory with an actual date on it. I liked seeing him proud of something nobody had asked him to perform.\n\n\"Mine too. I thought the floods were just getting worse.\"\n\n\"They may be,\" Fen said. \"But the blocked route has made the pattern less forgiving.\"\n\nNeri sat at the far end of the table with a stack of copied maintenance receipts. Their face was pale.\n\n\"The council kept repairing the surface channels,\" they said. \"We told ourselves we were improving the valley.\"\n\n\"You were repairing symptoms,\" Tamsin answered. \"Sometimes repairs were necessary. They weren't the whole plan.\"\n\nI felt a cold ache beneath my ribs.\n\nThe old decision had not ended when the keepers died or the records burned. It had become a pattern in the landscape, the kind people accepted because they had lived with it long enough.\n\nMaui stared at the map.\n\n\"There,\" he said, tapping the thick black line. \"Open it. Let the water go where it's trying to go.\"\n\nFen shot him a look. \"The last time somebody thought that, the passage closed on people.\"\n\n\"I know.\" His voice rose. \"That's why I want it open. Nobody should have to watch their house flood because someone a hundred years ago was afraid!\"\n\nMini Maui appeared on his chest, lifted a tiny imaginary gate, and immediately fell backward under the weight. Maui looked down and made an irritated sound.\n\n\"Great. Even my tattoos have joined the committee.\"\n\nToph put a hand on the table. \"You can lift a rock, big guy. You can't tell me whether the soil under it will hold.\"\n\nMaui's mouth opened. For a moment I expected another argument about his legendary strength. Then he stared at the garden marked beyond the line and shut it again.\n\n\"Fine. Tell me what you *can* check,\" he said to Fen. \"And then give me something heavy to do that won't make it worse.\"\n\nThe demand was still loud. The choice to listen was real. They could exist in the same sentence without erasing each other.\n\n\"Then open the barrier.\"\n\nFen shook their head.\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\n\"People are getting wet.\"\n\n\"And they'd get much wetter if an unstable tunnel failed under the spring.\"\n\nThe silence was uncomfortable.\n\nDanse laid out the inspection results from the upper chamber. The mortar was different in two sections. One support showed long-term movement. We could test the surface outlet and keep the temporary channels open, but the lower route required engineering work before any attempt at full reopening.\n\nI wanted to argue.\n\nInstead I asked what the current closure actually meant.\n\n\"The upper maintenance hatch can be opened under supervision for inspection,\" Tamsin said. \"The lower flood barrier remains closed. Surface drains need additional capacity before the next storm.\"\n\nEdda raised her hand.\n\n\"And the detour must be usable by people who can't take the steps to the upper ridge.\"\n\nWe rewrote the work list.\n\nThe council would fund an accessible temporary path and a monitored surface overflow. Tideglass would provide tide data, Riverstone would oversee drainage effects on gardens, Brasswatch would test the portable pump and new fittings, and Moonroot would preserve the old plans without claiming they gave anyone permission to enter unsafe spaces.\n\nThe work needed money and patience.\n\nNeither was popular.\n\nAfter the meeting, I found Neri by the fountain.\n\nThey were staring at the old stone arch.\n\n\"I used to think the sealed route was part of our history,\" they said. \"Something unfortunate and finished.\"\n\n\"It wasn't finished.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nThey looked at me.\n\n\"Do you hate me for not asking sooner?\"\n\nI had to think before answering.\n\n\"I'm angry that people kept suffering. I'm glad you're asking now. Both are true.\"\n\nThey nodded slowly.\n\n\"That is more than I expected.\"\n\nThe temporary channel was built over the following week. It ran along the safe upper path, using removable sections that could be cleaned without entering the old tunnel. Sela helped place the runoff markers near the garden. Edda tested the detour in wet weather. Toph checked the supports while Tamsin logged the movement.\n\nMaui carried materials when asked and learned the names of three workers who had previously been invisible to him. He brought hot food from the pantry after the late shift, then stayed to wash the pots.\n\nHalfway through installing the last removable section, the tide turned sooner than Fen had predicted. Water pressed against the temporary barrier and the nearest joint began to shiver.\n\nMaui planted his feet and caught the side beam before it could shift.\n\n“I've got it!” he shouted.\n\nToph felt the movement under her boots. “You have the beam. You don't have the foundation, Muscles.”\n\nEdda had already called for the people on the lower walk to move behind the marked line. Fen checked the tide gauge and told the crew to stop feeding more water through the test section.\n\n“Let me lift it,” Maui offered. “I can move the whole thing onto higher ground.”\n\nTamsin looked at the bolts he was holding. “If you lift now, you'll tear out the anchors on the other end.”\n\nFor an instant he seemed ready to argue. His hook flashed in his hand. I knew the kind of story he liked: one enormous effort, a magnificent rescue, cheers and a place beside the fire where everyone would repeat what he'd done.\n\nThen he lowered the hook.\n\n“Tell me what to hold,” he said.\n\nEdda directed him to support one beam while the workers released pressure from the inspection gate. Toph checked the ground, Tamsin resecured the movable brackets and Fen recorded the tide height at which the test had failed. I held the tools, dry enough to use, and tried not to think about how close the water had come to soaking the market stalls.\n\nWhen the barrier stopped moving, nobody cheered. The crew had to revise the safe operating range and postpone opening the upper route during high tide. A closed gate was frustrating. It was better than a false promise.\n\nMaui wiped saltwater from his forehead. “Well,” he said, “I only saved one beam.”\n\n“You listened to three people while saving it,” Toph said. “That's your new record.”\n\nHe looked offended for a moment. Then he grinned. “Someone write that down. I want it on a monument.”\n\nTamsin handed him the inspection sheet. “Sign here instead.”\n\nHe did, beneath the workers whose measurements had actually kept the channel standing.\n\nThe work did not make him smaller.\n\nI was beginning to think it made him more himself.\n\nOn the final test day, a controlled flow of water ran through the temporary channel and out toward the coast. Fen watched the gauges. Danse checked the pump. Sela stood beside the garden, waiting to see whether her soil would flood again.\n\nThe first test passed.\n\nThe second revealed a small leak near the lower joint.\n\nWe stopped and repaired it.\n\nThe third passed within the limits the team had written down.\n\nNobody cheered until Fen said the words.\n\n\"Safe for the posted conditions.\"\n\nThen we cheered.\n\nSela insisted on walking the team through the garden once the test ended. The new flow had missed the lowest bed, but a narrow strip of soil beside the path was damp in a way it hadn't been before.\n\n\"I want that measured tomorrow,\" she said. \"It may be nothing. My seedlings don't care whether we call it nothing.\"\n\nFen agreed and added the observation to the test sheet. Tamsin marked the difference between water that had been deliberately released during the controlled trial and rain they couldn't predict. Edda asked whether the temporary channel would still leave an accessible route if one section had to be removed for cleaning.\n\n\"We can test that,\" Danse answered.\n\n\"Before the next storm,\" she said.\n\nToph rested her feet against the path and made a face. \"At least the stone isn't shifting.\"\n\n\"Not shifting under the conditions we measured,\" Tamsin corrected.\n\nToph laughed. \"You engineers are contagious.\"\n\nMaui watched the exchange without offering to move the entire channel. I was absurdly proud of him.\n\nWhen the work stopped for a break, he sat beside me on a low wall.\n\n\"I was THIS close to announcing that I saved the entire valley,\" he admitted, spreading his arms. \"Then Sela mentioned her seedlings and Fen mentioned the gauges. Nobody understands the hard life of a humble legend.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"Is that wrong?\"\n\n\"No. It's a thing we want, not a fact we can claim yet.\"\n\nHe looked out toward Tideglass. \"You make hope sound like paperwork.\"\n\n\"Sometimes hope needs a place to put the numbers.\"\n\nHe smiled at that, and when I leaned against him his arm settled around me without pulling me away from the garden. We could care about the place and each other at the same time.\n\nI thought of the old gate beneath the Closed Bells line. It remained sealed for reasons we were still trying to understand. We didn't have to open it to prove the work we'd done mattered. For now, the new surface route had passed a limited test, and people who lived beside it had the right to question what came next.\n\nThe cheering stopped as soon as Fen asked us to record the test conditions.\n\n\"If the tide rises past the marked line, close the channel. If the debris screen blocks, close it until it's cleared. If nobody on duty answers, don't improvise.\"\n\nMaui rested on his shovel. \"You have an extraordinary talent for ruining celebrations.\"\n\n\"And keeping people alive for the next one,\" Fen answered.\n\nSela asked about frozen winter ground. Fen admitted the team had no winter data. Rather than declare permanent success, they wrote down what needed monitoring. Edda tested the route in the evening light and pointed out a marker that disappeared against the wet paving. Tamsin moved it, then asked her to try again.\n\nNeri posted three notices: the approved operating conditions, the name of each duty worker, and a private route for residents to report flooding. Nobody would have to post a home address or a medical need beside the bakery menu. Two people outside the repair crew would review disputes.\n\n\"That seems rather formal,\" Maui said.\n\n\"Water can be extremely formal when it reaches somebody's kitchen,\" Sela replied.\n\nEven Danse smiled.\n\nA child asked whether the new channel would stay forever. Fen crouched beside the diagram.\n\n\"We don't know. We test things, fix them, and sometimes replace them.\"\n\n\"So something can work without being finished?\"\n\n\"Especially that,\" Toph said.\n\nWe put the tools away before rain arrived. The unknown signature in Moonroot's Closed Bells records was still unknown, and no newly approved surface test gave us permission to cross the old underground barrier. But for tonight Sela could go home with a dry garden and Edda could use the path. That felt worth celebrating properly.\n\nThe lower passage remained closed.\n\nThat evening, Maui found me sitting on the bench above Tideglass. I was tired enough that the sea seemed to move in slow pieces.\n\n\"Disappointed?\" he asked.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"Because we didn't open the old route?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe sat beside me.\n\n\"Me too.\"\n\nI leaned against him.\n\n\"But the gardens are safer,\" I said.\n\n\"And people can use the path.\"\n\n\"And the inspection records are public.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"Not exactly a great legend.\"\n\n\"Perhaps we should stop measuring our lives by whether they make good legends.\"\n\nHe pretended to consider.\n\n\"That sounds like more work.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe kissed me softly.\n\nBelow us, the water moved through the new surface channel, doing what water had always tried to do.\n\nFinding somewhere safe to go.","word_count":2398},"maui-48":{"number":48,"title":"The Lantern Ledger","subtitle":"Lanternweald — sixth expansion pass","content":"The new ledger had no columns for loyalty.\n\nThat was Neri's first rule.\n\nWe sat at a long table in Moonroot Archive, comparing the surviving pages of the old arrival book with a clean blank volume. The old book had been designed for people who believed that crossing into Lanternweald created a debt. It recorded names, expected return dates, assigned keepers, and—in several places—marks that might have indicated whether someone had been permitted to leave.\n\nSome of those marks made my skin crawl.\n\n\"Are we keeping any of this?\" I asked.\n\nNeri touched the cover.\n\n\"We need to preserve the original as evidence. We do not need to make a new version of its authority.\"\n\nSeverus, working at the next desk, looked up.\n\n\"An excellent distinction.\"\n\nOrin had prepared a proposed format for the public record. It listed structural events, maintenance decisions, funding approvals, and testimony categories. Personal travel details would be kept only when someone requested help and only for as long as the service required. Romantic relationships were not part of the civic record.\n\nThe reading table had been lowered after Edda watched three visitors struggle to reach its writing surface. One leg was still propped on a wedge of scrap wood. Whenever Maui leaned forward, the ink bottle rattled.\n\n\"Stop attacking the furniture,\" Severus said.\n\n\"I'm reading,\" Maui replied.\n\n\"Then your technique requires revision.\"\n\nMaui stepped away with theatrical dignity. \"Observe. Flawless reading posture.\"\n\nOrin pressed the table's corner. It shifted anyway.\n\n\"The repair order is still open,\" they said. \"The record isn't public in any useful sense if half the people who need it can't write on the table.\"\n\nA charcoal line on the floor marked a turning space. Someone had moved the chair across it.\n\nI moved the chair back and fetched the order. One line specified the table leg, another a lamp that shone directly into a reader's eyes. Neither would reveal the erased keeper's name, but both mattered to the person who wanted to read the record.\n\nMaui raised his hook. \"I can hold the table steady all day.\"\n\n\"An admirable proposal,\" Severus said, \"if the archive intends to employ you as permanent furniture.\"\n\n\"Very handsome furniture.\"\n\n\"We have a maintenance budget,\" Orin said. \"We will use it.\"\n\nI noted the temporary assistance arrangement until the repair could be tested. Neri approved the sign. Severus returned to his evidence list. Maui put down his hook, apparently devastated that a perfectly good heroic job had been assigned to a carpenter.\n\n\"Nobody appreciates an architectural legend,\" he muttered.\n\n\"Nobody wants to write on one,\" I said.\n\nI wanted to hug the person who'd written that sentence.\n\nInstead I told Orin it was good work.\n\nThey looked delighted.\n\nThe hardest part was the blank line.\n\nIt belonged to the erased signature from the Closed Bells ledger. The investigation had documented the order, the materials, the closure, and Neri's witness account of a person denied departure. But the name on the original page was gone, and none of the surviving evidence established it with sufficient confidence.\n\nAvel, the councilor who had demanded a name before, came to review the draft.\n\n\"You're leaving the blank?\" he asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri said.\n\n\"People will think you're protecting someone.\"\n\n\"Then the note beside it should explain exactly why it's blank,\" Severus answered.\n\nI turned the page around so Avel could read it.\n\n*Signature removed from the surviving document. Identity unverified. The harm caused by the closure is documented independently. Additional evidence may be submitted without mandatory public disclosure.*\n\nAvel read it twice.\n\nHe looked tired.\n\n\"I want someone to answer for what happened.\"\n\n\"So do I,\" I said. \"That doesn't give us permission to choose a person because the empty space makes us angry.\"\n\nHe sat down.\n\nFor a moment I thought he might begin another argument. Instead he asked to see the evidence list.\n\nWe showed him the invoices, tide charts, witness summary, and the notes explaining what each could and could not prove. We also showed him the emergency policy changes already enacted because of the investigation: voluntary departure routes, clear access to the upper hall, no public lists of destinations, and reviewable decisions during closures.\n\nHe studied them.\n\n\"This is more than a blank,\" he said finally.\n\n\"Yes,\" Neri answered. \"It is the work around it.\"\n\nBefore we released the draft, Orin caught a problem in the access rules.\n\nThe next morning a traveler came to the archive with a small folded ticket and a question about the entry for their family. They didn't want their destination made public. They wanted to know whether the date on the record could be corrected without showing everyone where they'd gone.\n\nNeri invited them to sit at the lower reading table. The table had been repaired the previous week, but the lamp above it still needed a shade. Orin moved the lamp rather than asking the traveler to change seats.\n\n“May I see the original ticket?” Neri asked.\n\nThe traveler hesitated, then passed it over. The name on the card matched the restricted register; the time did not. The paper was stained with salt, and I could see why a tired clerk might have read the figures incorrectly.\n\n“Will the correction be visible?” the traveler asked.\n\n“The fact that a correction occurred, yes,” Neri answered. “Not your private route. The public ledger will show a dated amendment to the timetable, and the restricted file will keep the ticket with the reason.”\n\nThey thought about that. “Who gets to see the restricted version?”\n\n“Only the archive staff authorized to handle it, unless you give specific permission otherwise.”\n\nMaui stood behind me with the parcel of blank forms he'd carried from the printer. He was unusually quiet. Mini Maui had started drawing a little heroic stamp on his forearm, but the real Maui covered it with two fingers.\n\n“Good,” the traveler said. “Then you can write it.”\n\nOrin entered the correction while they watched. The old date stayed legible, crossed with a single line and signed beside the new one. No magic erased the original mistake, and no one acted as though admitting it had ruined the archive.\n\nAfter they left, Maui set down his parcel.\n\n“Do you think they're happy?” he asked.\n\n“I don't know. They got what they asked for.”\n\n“That is a very small victory.”\n\n“Small for you.”\n\nHe looked at the corrected date, then at the empty space where the private destination had not been copied. “Right,” he said. And he went to fetch another stack of forms without trying to make himself the hero of the story.\n\n\"A public correction,\" they said, tracing the margin, \"might reproduce somebody's private statement if we're not careful. Suppose a person gives us an account in confidence and later asks us to correct the date. Do we announce the whole account to prove we fixed it?\"\n\nNeri looked at the page.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Then the correction procedure needs two paths,\" Orin said. \"One for public evidence and one for confidential testimony.\"\n\nSeverus put down his pen.\n\n\"That is not a minor adjustment.\"\n\nOrin's face fell.\n\n\"It's a necessary one,\" he continued. \"My objection is to how easily we could have overlooked it.\"\n\nWe drew two parallel columns. Public documents could receive a dated correction note that cited the original. Confidential statements would be reviewed with their contributor, with only an appropriately limited public summary when the contributor agreed. Emergency information could be used for safety without turning a frightened traveler's identity into festival gossip.\n\nA visitor from Tideglass arrived with a damaged invoice. They wanted to know whether the bill had been paid before the old closure, but they did not want their family's name displayed.\n\n\"Can you check without publishing it?\" they asked.\n\nNeri explained the process we'd just written.\n\n\"Good,\" the visitor said. \"Then I'd like you to look.\"\n\nThe transaction was small, one box of hinges. It did not name the missing keeper or resolve the Closed Bells. Orin recorded what it did establish and carefully marked the rest unknown.\n\nI felt a strange relief. The blank signature wasn't the only place where uncertainty mattered. Every small record needed a way for people to challenge it without surrendering the privacy the new rules were meant to protect.\n\nWhen the visitor left, Severus looked at Orin.\n\n\"You have improved the procedure.\"\n\nOrin smiled.\n\n\"By finding a mistake?\"\n\n\"That is generally how improvement begins,\" he said.\n\nMaui, carrying the empty paper wrapping from the printer, leaned in.\n\n\"You need an expert in mistakes? Lucky day. I've made some legendary ones. None came with such spectacularly boring handwriting, but I can adapt.\"\n\n\"Please refrain,\" Severus answered.\n\nI laughed, and Neri added the correction policy to the first page where every reader could see it.\n\nThe ledger was opened to public review the following week.\n\nPeople came with questions, corrections, and memories. Some brought documents; others asked whether they could give testimony privately. One person declined to leave a name and was told that was their choice. Another requested a copy of the revised emergency rules for a homeworld council dealing with flood evacuations.\n\nNeri gave them the public version.\n\nNobody received keys to private files merely because they were curious.\n\nMaui came in during the second afternoon, carrying a parcel of paper from the printer. He set it on the table and looked at the ledger.\n\n\"A ledger without a column for the biggest hero in the room?\" he asked, setting down the parcel. \"I demand a recount.\"\n\n\"There isn't a contest,\" Neri said.\n\n\"Then why am I losing?\"\n\nMini Maui drew an enormous star on his chest, struck it out, and pointed to the unfilled private record. For a moment Maui's expression turned thoughtful.\n\n\"People used to write down who belonged to whom?\" he asked.\n\n\"Who had authority to send travelers away or keep them here,\" Neri said.\n\nHis jaw tightened. He could joke about a competition, but the idea of somebody being trapped by a mark in a book seemed to anger him in a way the missing line never had.\n\n\"All right,\" he said, a little too loudly. \"New rule. If somebody wants to go, you let them go. If they want to come back, you ask whether they want company. If they want bread—\"\n\n\"Mara handles bread,\" I interrupted.\n\n\"She does. Very well. The great Maui merely delivers it.\"\n\nHe grinned again, but he didn't try to fill the blank line. It wasn't his story to complete.\n\n\"That's the famous blank line?\"\n\n\"Yes,\" I said.\n\nHe leaned closer.\n\n\"Looks smaller than I imagined.\"\n\n\"Most important things do.\"\n\nHe grinned.\n\nSeverus made a disapproving noise.\n\n\"Please do not encourage him.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nLater, when the visitors had gone, I found Neri standing alone by the display case.\n\n\"How are you?\" I asked.\n\nThey considered.\n\n\"Less certain than I once was. More responsible, perhaps.\"\n\n\"That sounds exhausting.\"\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nThey smiled faintly.\n\n\"I thought telling the truth would finally let me stop carrying it.\"\n\n\"Did it?\"\n\n\"No. But now other people can help carry the work.\"\n\nI looked at the new ledger.\n\nThe old book would remain in the archive, preserved with its flaws. The new one would be revised when evidence changed or policies failed. Its pages were numbered, its handling rules public, and its blank lines explained instead of quietly filled by whoever held the pen.\n\nMaui waited for me at the arch.\n\n\"Ready to go home?\" he asked.\n\n\"In a minute.\"\n\nI wrote one final note in the green notebook.\n\n*Accountability is not a perfect story. It is a record that lets other people ask questions tomorrow.*\n\nI closed it.\n\nThen I took Maui's hand and went through the open passage toward the square, where the lanterns were coming on one by one.\n\nThe next day a young visitor came back with a correction.\n\nTheir family's crossing had been entered as a departure at dusk, but the ferry hadn't sailed until the following morning. The public map used that time to estimate the last safe crossing, and the error made the old route look more reliable than it was.\n\nOrin found the source note.\n\n\"You're right,\" they said. \"The time was copied from the departure request, not the actual tide record.\"\n\nNeri asked whether the visitor wanted to attach a private statement or have the correction attributed anonymously.\n\n\"Anonymous, please.\"\n\n\"Then that's what we'll do.\"\n\nThe public page received a dated correction and a link to the weather record. Nobody wrote the visitor's name beside it. The next ferry schedule carried the revised time.\n\nI watched the process with a feeling I couldn't quite name. We had spent so much time worrying about a missing historical signature that I'd forgotten how much a smaller error could matter to somebody trying to get home.\n\nSeverus came to inspect the update. He checked the reference, nodded once, and said, \"A better ledger is one that can be corrected without a trial.\"\n\nOrin grinned.\n\nMaui leaned over my shoulder.\n\n\"And one that doesn't expect me to understand every column?\"\n\n\"That may be asking too much,\" I said.\n\nHe laughed.\n\nThere were ordinary lives on both sides of every line we kept. That was what the new book had to remember.\n\nThe ledger stayed where it belonged.\n\nNot beneath a crown.\n\nOn a table that could be reached.","word_count":2226},"maui-05":{"number":5,"title":"Where the Water Goes","subtitle":"The Shore I Choose · Chapter 5 of 10","content":"Maui's fishhook could move stone, calm a snarled rope, and turn its owner into an alarming number of animals. It could not persuade a tiny brass compass to point north.\n\n“Defective,” he announced, waving the spinning compass like evidence at a trial. “I've wrestled monsters, raised islands, and argued with the sun. Apparently my greatest enemy is a needle the size of a shrimp.”\n\n“You keep holding it beside the hook.”\n\n“Which should make it grateful.”\n\nThe compass was Neri's. It had begun spinning three days earlier, when a Threadway opened at the foot of Tideglass Cove and let in a cold tide from somewhere none of us recognized. The current was taking sand from the beach and pulling it toward a crack beneath the rocks.\n\nIf it continued, the little harbor would lose its shelter.\n\nMaui wanted to plug the crack with a boulder. I wanted to map the current first.\n\nWe argued on the shore while seabirds wheeled overhead.\n\n“You're making a plan for a puddle,” he said.\n\n“It's an inlet with moving water.”\n\n“Very threatening water.”\n\n“It might be, if you stop pretending the only answer is being bigger than the problem.”\n\nHis face closed.\n\nI regretted the sharpness before I had finished speaking, but I did not apologize for the idea.\n\n“I'm going to measure it,” I said. “You can help, or not.”\n\nI took string, weights, and strips of colored cloth down the beach. After a long silence, Maui picked up the heaviest bundle.\n\n“Tell me where,” he said.\n\nFor two hours we tracked the current. The water was not trying to drag the shore away. It was being pulled through a doorway beneath the rocks, one that had opened unevenly and become stuck.\n\nMaui knelt beside the opening.\n\n“So. Boulder's a bad idea.”\n\n“Spectacularly.”\n\nHe gave me a narrow look. “Enjoy this moment. It is rare.”\n\nWe could not decide what to call the doorway.\n\nNeri said *tide arch*, because the old survey marked the waterline beside it. Maui said *the world's worst bathtub plug*, which was not a name I intended to write in the records. I called it *the lower Tideglass Threadway* on my first page and then underlined *provisional* beside the name.\n\nFen, the tidekeeper, met us on the sand with a weighted cord and a sour expression.\n\n\"You're both standing where the bank slipped yesterday,\" they said.\n\nMaui looked at the sand beneath his feet. \"This bank?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe moved. I followed him, somewhat less proudly.\n\nFen showed us yesterday's marks: a dark line left by high tide, a fan of pebbles where the current had turned, and a narrow groove cut through the sediment. Whatever the unstable opening was doing, it wasn't merely making a compass misbehave. It was redirecting the water that sheltered fishing boats.\n\n\"If we block it,\" Fen said, \"the pressure will look for another weakness. If we leave it, the sand will move out of the harbor.\"\n\nMaui frowned. \"So both choices are bad.\"\n\n\"Unmeasured choices are bad,\" Fen replied. \"Let's find one we can understand.\"\n\nI liked Fen immediately.\n\nMaui crouched beside the safer of the two tide pools and watched the foam for a moment. The showman's grin slipped into a look I knew from watching him read a sail.\n\n\"That water's curling the wrong way,\" he said.\n\nFen glanced up. \"Which part?\"\n\n\"The little return flow along the black rock. The wave comes in from the south, but the bubbles go north before the rest of the water falls back.\"\n\nI followed his pointing finger. He was right. I would never have seen it through the glare.\n\n\"That's useful,\" Fen said. \"It may mean there's another hollow under the ledge. We should mark it and check when the tide drops.\"\n\nMaui beamed. \"The great Maui: discoverer of bubbles.\"\n\n\"Please don't carve that into the rock,\" I said.\n\n\"It would be an excellent inscription. Very historically significant.\"\n\nMini Maui appeared over his shoulder and drew an imaginary crown around a single bubble. I tried not to laugh.\n\n\"You're good at this,\" I admitted.\n\n\"At everything, technically.\"\n\n\"At reading water.\"\n\nHis expression shifted. For all his bragging, he seemed to need the distinction as much as the praise. \"I spent a lot of nights watching the sea because I didn't have anyone to talk to. You learn which way trouble is moving.\"\n\nFen made a note beside the current reading. They did not mistake his observation for a full survey or abandon the measured plan.\n\nWhen he stood, he offered his hand to help me onto the dry rock. His palm was warm and callused. I kept it a second longer than the climb required.\n\n\"Careful,\" he murmured. \"You're staring at the greatest bubble expert alive.\"\n\n\"Terrible title.\"\n\n\"Then you'll have to help me improve it.\"\n\nWe tied strips of blue and red cloth to separate lengths of line and set them in the current from a safe point on the rock. The red strip swung toward the inlet and stayed there; the blue one tugged outward, catching a counterflow against the submerged stone. I recorded their movements while Fen called tide intervals. Maui held the weighted rope steady with a care I hadn't expected.\n\nMy pencil broke.\n\n\"Of course,\" I muttered.\n\nMaui handed me a charcoal stub from his pouch.\n\n\"You carry charcoal?\" I asked.\n\n\"For writing my own magnificent praises.\"\n\n\"Naturally.\"\n\n\"Also for fire-starting.\"\n\n\"Less magnificent.\"\n\n\"Not when you're cold.\"\n\nI drew a second diagram on the back of Neri's discarded receipt. The pattern made sense: the bent ring was keeping one side of the arch open. Water entered at the wrong moment, not as an ordinary tide but as a narrow, powerful crosscurrent. The harbor did not need a heroic boulder. It needed that hinge seated correctly, and it needed a way to check that the fix held after the tide changed.\n\nFen studied the sketch.\n\n\"Can you reach the ring without standing in the pull?\"\n\n\"Only if Maui lifts the outer slab,\" I said. \"And only if you keep the line attached to both of us.\"\n\nMaui opened his mouth to object to needing a rope.\n\nMini Maui marched down his forearm, wrapped an imaginary line around his own waist, and pointed at Fen's safety equipment with such fierce approval that I laughed.\n\n\"You're both enjoying this,\" Maui complained. \"One little rope and suddenly the guy who wrangled the sun needs a chaperone.\"\n\n\"Not a chaperone,\" Fen said. \"An anchor that can hold when somebody slips.\"\n\n\"A very handsome anchor?\"\n\n\"Rated for the load.\"\n\nHis grin faltered, then returned with more determination. \"Fine. Rated for the load. But if someone writes a song about this, I expect a better rhyme than *rope*.\"\n\nHe clipped in at the point Fen indicated. When the slab shifted he held its full weight without pretending that meant the current would obey him. The hook could change his shape; it couldn't change the water's next decision.\n\nFen raised an eyebrow.\n\nHe closed it.\n\n\"Fine,\" he said. \"A very heroic rope.\"\n\nThe first attempt failed. The slab shifted, grit slid across the ring, and I had to pull my hand back before the metal caught my sleeve. Maui held the weight while Fen called a halt.\n\n\"Again?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"Not until we check the tether,\" Fen replied.\n\nI was shaking. It took me an embarrassing moment to admit it.\n\n\"Could we stop for five minutes?\" I asked.\n\nMaui looked at me. \"Of course.\"\n\nDuring the break, Fen unrolled a second chart across the rock. It showed where the current ran at low tide and how far it shifted when the cold Threadway water pushed through.\n\n\"There's a difference between a temporary surge and a changed channel,\" they said. \"If we force that ring now, we might get through the first one and still leave the harbor worse next week.\"\n\nI traced the lines with a finger. The hand that had drawn the little lantern bracket the day before wanted to leap ahead to a solution. My legs were trembling from the first attempt.\n\n\"Can we compare it with yesterday's waterline?\" I asked.\n\nFen pointed to a chalk mark under the rock ledge. \"That's why we measured it.\"\n\nMaui crouched beside us. He looked at the chalk, then at the boulder he'd suggested using as a plug.\n\n\"So if I block the opening, the water finds somewhere else.\"\n\n\"Possibly through somebody's landing,\" Fen said.\n\nHe sighed. \"My boulder has become unpopular.\"\n\nI wanted to tease him. Instead I passed him the rope and asked if he'd help mark the next safe position.\n\nHe tied one knot, then stopped. \"This kind?\"\n\n\"Show Fen.\"\n\nFen checked it and made him redo the loose turn. He didn't argue. When the rope finally held the strain under a controlled pull, he looked delighted in a quieter way than I'd ever seen.\n\n\"Is this what ordinary sailors do?\" he asked.\n\n\"Among other things,\" Fen said.\n\n\"Exhausting.\"\n\nI laughed, and the tension in my shoulders eased. We still had a dangerous mechanism to inspect. Nobody had made it safe just by being brave. But we had a measured window, a better tether, and permission to stop when the conditions changed.\n\nWhen we tried again, I counted aloud rather than letting everyone imagine they knew what I was thinking. Maui waited for the count. That, more than his strength, made me trust the attempt.\n\nI sat on the high dry rock and drank water. My hands had remembered the shape of the mechanism, but they still belonged to a person who could be frightened. Nobody called me weak. Fen marked the next safe window, and Maui sat beside me without turning my pause into a lecture.\n\n\"Still think the boulder would work?\" I asked.\n\n\"Absolutely,\" he said. \"For making a larger disaster.\"\n\nI laughed, then felt brave enough to stand again.\n\nOn the second attempt, we reset the ring a fraction at a time. I asked Maui to shift the stone; he waited for my instruction. Fen watched the tide marks and gave us room to stop whenever the water changed. When the blue cloth released from the pull, all three of us saw it.\n\n\"Hold,\" Fen called.\n\nWe held.\n\nThe ring settled. The line slackened.\n\nWe didn't celebrate until Fen finished the last measurement.\n\nWe worked together. He shifted the rocks enough for me to reach the bent brass ring at the heart of the door. I adjusted it, checked where the pressure would go, and used the blue thread from my old key to mark the safe alignment. When Maui returned the last stone, the inlet took a breath and settled.\n\nThe compass stopped spinning.\n\nAt sunset, we sat above the cove with sand in our shoes. I nudged his shoulder.\n\n“You didn't have to let me win the argument.”\n\n“I didn't.”\n\nI looked at him.\n\n“You were right,” he said. “That's different.”\n\nThe admission cost him something. I could see it in the way his hand tightened around the hook.\n\n“Does being wrong hurt?” I asked.\n\nHe laughed without humor. “When everyone expects you to save them? Yes.”\n\nI thought of the voice from my memory, the one that had taught me usefulness was a debt.\n\n“I know that feeling,” I said.\n\nFor a while we watched the water go where it was meant to go.\n\nThen Maui asked whether I wanted him to teach me to steer by stars that changed their positions every night. I asked whether he could actually do that.\n\n“No,” he said. “But it sounds like a magnificent challenge.”\n\nI took his offered hand to stand.\n\nFor once the horizon didn't seem to be demanding that either of us prove we deserved to be standing in front of it.","word_count":1963},"maui-27":{"number":27,"title":"The Visitor's Right to Leave","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fifth expansion pass","content":"The boy was sitting on his suitcase when I found him.\n\nHe couldn't have been more than nineteen, though his boots were worn in the way boots get when someone has been walking without much choice about where. His name was Tomas. He'd arrived through the northern Threadway three days ago and spent most of that time helping at the pantry. Now he was waiting on the steps beneath the arch.\n\nBeside him stood a woman named Vel, who had started calling Maui the Guardian of Tideglass after seeing him catch a boat during the mooring accident.\n\n\"You don't understand,\" Vel was telling him. \"People made room for you here. You can't just leave after they've helped.\"\n\nTomas stared at his shoes.\n\n\"I need to go home.\"\n\n\"Then why come at all?\"\n\nI stopped close enough to be heard without coming between them.\n\n\"Because people are allowed to visit,\" I said.\n\nVel turned toward me.\n\n\"The community fed him.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And gave him a bed.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"And now he just walks out?\"\n\nI looked at Tomas.\n\n\"Do you want help reaching your door?\"\n\nHe nodded without looking up.\n\n\"All right. I'll check the safe route.\"\n\nVel put a hand on the suitcase handle.\n\n\"Someone should tell Maui.\"\n\nI felt my shoulders tighten.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"He's the one who welcomed the newcomers. He'll understand.\"\n\nTomas pulled his suitcase back.\n\n\"Please don't make me explain to him.\"\n\nIt was barely louder than the sea.\n\nI took a breath. Then I asked Vel to release the handle. She did, reluctantly.\n\n\"Neri has a private departure service,\" I said. \"No one has to explain their reasons to a crowd.\"\n\nVel looked hurt.\n\n\"I thought this was a place where people cared.\"\n\n\"It is. Caring doesn't make the doors ours.\"\n\nI escorted Tomas to the notice board. The departure instructions were clear, but somebody had hung a festival banner across half the text. I moved it aside. Neri was on duty and listened as Tomas asked for a route without giving an address.\n\n\"Would you like a guide to the arch?\" they asked.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Would you like us to keep your destination private?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Then that's what we'll do.\"\n\nMaui arrived before we left, striding down the steps with his hook over one shoulder as though the ferry departure were an adventure he'd been promised. He saw Tomas's packed suitcase, and the grin he had brought for the crowd faltered. \"Okay,\" he said, turning the hook over in his hands. \"Tell me which part of this requires an enormous hero. I may need the answer slowly.\"\n\nVel straightened when she saw him. I saw the moment he recognized that look: pure, uncomplicated admiration, the kind he'd chased across oceans.\n\n\"Guardian of Tideglass,\" she said. \"Tell him he can't just take everything and go.\"\n\nMaui drew himself to full height. Mini Maui struck a triumphant pose over his heart. For half a second the temptation was visible. A grand speech, a grateful crowd, an audience that knew his name.\n\nThen he saw Tomas's hands clenched around the suitcase.\n\n\"Whoa,\" he said. \"Hold on. I rescue people. I don't collect them.\"\n\nVel frowned. \"But you made this place safe.\"\n\n\"With about a hundred other people, and several very angry clipboards.\" He grinned, then let the grin fade. \"That doesn't give me a vote on where Tomas goes next.\"\n\nMini Maui lowered his arms and looked mildly disappointed. Maui looked more disappointed still. He'd wanted to be somebody's guardian; being the person who opened a path without knowing whether he'd see them again was harder.\n\nWhen Tomas thanked him, Maui made a spectacular show of accepting the compliment. He didn't ask for a promise in return.\n\nNot because I'd summoned him. Vel had.\n\nHe came down the square with his hook over one shoulder and a confused expression.\n\n\"What's happening?\"\n\nVel stepped toward him.\n\n\"Tell him he should stay. We welcomed him.\"\n\nMaui looked at the suitcase, then at Tomas, then at me.\n\nI didn't rescue him from the choice.\n\n\"Tomas,\" he said, \"do you want to go home?\"\n\nTomas nodded.\n\n\"Then do you need anything for the journey?\"\n\nVel stared.\n\n\"But the pantry—\"\n\n\"The pantry helped because he needed help,\" Maui said. \"Not because we bought another member of the village.\"\n\nHe said it plainly. No thunder. No boast.\n\nVel looked at him like he'd broken something she loved.\n\n\"I thought you understood loyalty.\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"Then why aren't you asking him to stay?\"\n\n\"Because I wouldn't want someone to keep me somewhere by calling it gratitude.\"\n\nTomas looked up for the first time.\n\n\"Thank you,\" he whispered.\n\nWe walked him to the arch along the safe route. Neri came too, carrying the travel map. The Threadway glowed faintly in the stone, not bright enough for a spectacle. Tomas put his suitcase down, checked that he had his papers, and asked whether he could come back someday.\n\nBefore the final bend, Edda's temporary marker showed where a cart had scraped the bridge railing. A painted arrow pointed away from the damaged edge.\n\nMaui reached for Tomas's suitcase.\n\nTomas held on.\n\n\"Sorry,\" Maui said, drawing back. \"I was going to perform an astonishingly efficient carry.\"\n\n\"I know. I want to carry it myself.\"\n\nMaui turned to me. \"This village has conspired against my best tricks.\"\n\n\"Only the ones involving somebody else's luggage.\"\n\nHe groaned, then stepped onto the clear side of the path and made a sweeping bow. \"Your route, honored traveler. Free scenery. Unbelievably handsome guide. No obligation to tip.\"\n\nTomas smiled without surrendering the handle.\n\nNeri checked the printed map and found that it still showed the old, wider crossing. They penciled the correction and left a note for Edda.\n\n\"Does every path come with paperwork?\" Maui demanded.\n\n\"Only the paths people use,\" Neri said.\n\n\"Then I'll never be able to retire.\"\n\nTomas had been watching the revised map while the rest of us argued about ink. He reached out and tapped the place where the path crossed the old terrace.\n\n\"Can I go this way without telling the village where I'm going afterward?\"\n\nNeri lowered the register. \"Yes. The crossing record needs a date and confirmation that the route was open. The destination stays in the restricted record.\"\n\n\"Who sees that?\"\n\n\"Only the workers who need it to keep the doorway safe.\"\n\nTomas looked toward the arch. \"I might come back. I don't want that written like a promise.\"\n\n\"Then it won't be,\" Neri said.\n\nMaui opened his mouth, probably to offer an escort that would involve three heroic poses and a lecture about his magnificent sense of direction. He caught my expression and narrowed his eyes at me instead.\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"Nothing.\"\n\n\"I can restrain myself. Watch. A miracle.\"\n\nMini Maui performed an extravagant bow across his chest, complete with an invisible curtain.\n\nTomas laughed. It was the first uncomplicated sound I'd heard from him since I found him sitting on his suitcase.\n\n\"If you come back,\" Maui said, \"we'll still have the terrible rope by the landing.\"\n\n\"And the bread?\"\n\n\"The bread is good. Unlike the rope.\"\n\nTomas adjusted the suitcase handle. He didn't have to tell us whether he expected to return. The doorway was still a route, not an oath, and his next decision would belong to him.\n\nWe waited while Tomas crossed at his own pace. The arch would not vanish because no one rushed him. Maui followed beside me with his hands conspicuously empty.\n\nWhen Tomas paused to check his documents, Maui looked toward the door and then deliberately looked away, giving him the privacy of a man who had traveled far enough to know his own destination.\n\n\"If you want,\" Neri said.\n\nHe looked at Maui.\n\nMaui smiled.\n\n\"We'll still have terrible rope and decent bread.\"\n\nTomas laughed.\n\nThen he stepped through.\n\nThe light settled. The arch remained open for other travelers.\n\nVel had followed us at a distance. When Tomas disappeared, she turned away without speaking.\n\nAfter Tomas left, Neri took the public arrival register back to the office. They didn't cross out his name as though he'd never been there. They marked the departure as confirmed, with no destination printed for curious strangers.\n\nA small girl came up to the notice and asked whether Tomas had gone away because he didn't like the village.\n\n\"Maybe he missed home,\" I said. \"He was allowed to go even if he liked us very much.\"\n\nShe considered that. \"My grandmother goes home after tea.\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Nobody gets mad at her.\"\n\n\"Exactly.\"\n\nThe girl ran off toward the bakery. I wished I could explain the matter as simply to the adults.\n\nMaui watched Neri lock the restricted ledger.\n\n\"I almost told Tomas he should stay until the next feast,\" he said.\n\n\"Would that have made him happier?\"\n\n\"I don't know. It would have made me feel better.\"\n\nThat was a difficult admission. I took his hand while we walked toward the river path.\n\n\"You're allowed to miss someone after they leave,\" I said. \"You don't get to make the missing into a duty they owe you.\"\n\nHe rubbed his thumb across my knuckles. \"Does that apply when you travel to see other people you love?\"\n\n\"Yes. I've told you I'm ethically non-monogamous and prefer polyamory. That doesn't mean I'll never be sad when we part. It means we can be honest without turning the sadness into a chain.\"\n\n\"I might need to be reminded.\"\n\n\"You can ask.\"\n\nWe passed Vel beside the market. She was still angry, and I was still angry about what she'd said to Tomas. I didn't approach her to demand an apology on his behalf. She had a right to understand her part in it at her own pace, even if I wished she'd start immediately.\n\nAt home, Maui put the returned carving on the shelf. I watched him choose not to throw it away.\n\n\"What if Tomas never comes back?\" he asked.\n\n\"Then the carving can still remind you that you cared.\"\n\nHe nodded. The little fish sat crookedly against the wall, plainly unfinished. Neither of us turned it around to make it look perfect.\n\nMaui watched her go.\n\n\"That hurt her,\" he said.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"I wanted her to understand.\"\n\n\"She might. Later. Or she might not.\"\n\nHe stared at the stones.\n\n\"I used to think having people admire me meant I had done something good. Now I know I can do something good and lose their admiration anyway.\"\n\nI touched his hand.\n\n\"That isn't a comfortable lesson.\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\nWe returned to Tideglass at dusk. Fen was setting up the tide chart for the next morning, and the first lanterns were flickering along the ridge. Maui offered to help carry the stand. Fen accepted without ceremony.\n\nFor a while that was enough.\n\nThe next day Vel arrived at our door with the little wooden fish Maui had once given her. She held it out.\n\n\"I don't want this anymore,\" she said.\n\nMaui took it.\n\n\"I'm sorry it upset you.\"\n\n\"It didn't upset me. It made me think you were someone who would always protect us.\"\n\n\"I will help when I can.\"\n\n\"That isn't the same.\"\n\n\"No,\" he said.\n\nShe left.\n\nHe turned the fish over in his hand. It was badly carved, with one eye much larger than the other. I remembered him making it on a rainy afternoon and declaring it a masterpiece.\n\n\"Want me to tell you she was wrong?\" I asked.\n\nHe shook his head.\n\n\"Not right now.\"\n\nWe sat on the steps.\n\n\"I liked being the person she thought I was,\" he admitted. \"Even though he didn't exist.\"\n\nI leaned against his shoulder.\n\n\"People can be disappointed when you don't belong to their story.\"\n\n\"Even you?\"\n\n\"Especially me. That's why I have to keep asking what you want instead of deciding what your legend means.\"\n\nHe put the fish beside the door.\n\n\"Do you still want me?\"\n\nI turned toward him.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Even without a crowd?\"\n\n\"Preferably without a crowd.\"\n\nHe laughed, the sound small and tired.\n\nI kissed him, gently, then pulled back when he seemed to need room to breathe.\n\nThat evening he put the returned fish on the shelf beside the green notebook from my old room. Two objects from people who had wanted different things. Neither one had to be thrown away to make the lesson disappear.\n\nIn the morning I checked the departure notice.\n\nThe festival banner had been moved to another wall.\n\nSomeone had written beneath the instructions, in ordinary dark ink:\n\nLeaving is not a debt.\n\nI didn't know who had written it.\n\nI was glad someone had.","word_count":2102},"maui-45":{"number":45,"title":"The Last Big Performance","subtitle":"Lanternweald — sixth expansion pass","content":"The invitation was written in letters three inches high.\n\nMAUI, HERO OF THE TIDE, WILL OPEN THE REPAIR CELEBRATION.\n\nHe stared at the poster with an expression I couldn't read.\n\n\"Well,\" I said.\n\n\"Well.\"\n\nI waited for him to lift his chin and begin planning a speech about himself.\n\nInstead he asked, \"Who decided that?\"\n\nPip appeared from behind the lantern table.\n\n\"The entertainment committee. They thought people would like something exciting after all the repairs.\"\n\nMaui looked around the square.\n\nHis grin flickered over the poster. For a breath I saw the old hunger in his face: the gigantic letters, the crowd calling his name, everybody waiting to see what he would do.\n\nMini Maui struck a heroic pose on his chest and began counting imaginary cheers.\n\nMaui covered him with one hand.\n\n\"Okay,\" he muttered. \"I am not going to pretend this isn't flattering.\"\n\nPip raised an eyebrow. \"But?\"\n\n\"But somebody's got to carry the benches, the kids have their show, and I am fairly certain Fen will throw me into the sea if I set off fireworks near the rigging.\"\n\n\"You could still introduce the event,\" I said.\n\nMaui considered it. \"I could. With a dramatic speech, a magnificent transformation, perhaps an interpretive dance—\"\n\n\"No fireworks,\" Pip interrupted.\n\n\"Your people are relentless.\"\n\nHe laughed, and I loved that he could admit wanting the spotlight without making it the condition for helping. He was still a demigod who liked to hear his own name. The difference was that he could let other names be said beside it.\n\n\"Tell me where the benches go,\" he said. \"Then we'll talk about the interpretive dance.\"\n\nThe temporary surface channel had passed its second inspection. Riverstone's bridge was almost ready for its final load test. The western Starwake boards had reopened, and the Brasswatch generator had stopped behaving like an offended dragon. The village had survived a string of storms and repairs, but the lower service passage remained closed while engineers studied its age and the old decisions surrounding it.\n\nThere was reason to celebrate.\n\nThere was also a lot of work that didn't belong to one famous man.\n\nThe festival map had become a patchwork of little promises. Mara's bakery offered a bread-tasting table with a written ingredient list. Fen was setting up a knot-tying demonstration beside the dry path, nowhere near an active mooring. At Starwake, Greg had agreed to two songs, provided the stage crew had its own paid shifts. Mina was lending teacups for Iroh's tasting, on the condition that somebody counted them before and after.\n\nPip had drawn tiny symbols beside the activities. A painted fish marked the children’s paper-boat race in a shallow basin. A star marked the borrowed-song corner, where anyone could leave a melody title for the musicians to consider without expecting an immediate performance. A lantern marked the public notice board, with its blank space for residents who wanted to help at the next inspection.\n\n\"This is becoming a very elaborate party,\" I said.\n\nMaui leaned over my shoulder. \"Needs a contest.\"\n\n\"There's a knot contest.\"\n\n\"With prizes?\"\n\n\"Applause and a painted shell.\"\n\n\"Insufficient. I offer a personal congratulation from the greatest demigod in existence.\"\n\n\"That would be a punishment for some people.\"\n\nHe made an affronted noise, then grinned when I laughed. He caught my hand under the edge of the poster where Pip couldn't see.\n\n\"Later,\" he said quietly, \"I want one dance that nobody puts on a program.\"\n\nThe sudden softness made me forget my next joke.\n\n\"Deal,\" I said. \"But you don't get to pick the music without asking me.\"\n\nHe put a hand to his heart. \"A cruel restriction on artistic genius.\"\n\nPip called him to move a stack of benches. He went, swagger and all. The private dance could wait until the people who had come to enjoy the celebration could actually reach their seats.\n\n\"I want that poster framed,\" Maui said. \"Possibly in a larger frame. Have you seen the lettering?\"\n\nPip blinked. \"So you'll open?\"\n\nHe stared at the children's program, then at the empty space for the repair crew's names.\n\n\"I want to. That's the problem. I can hear myself stealing the morning before they've even begun.\"\n\n\"Are you sure?\" Pip asked.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI didn't answer for him.\n\n\"Ask the children if they want an introduction,\" he said. \"If they do, I'll make it their big entrance. If they don't, I'll heroically survive sitting down.\"\n\nThe disappointment was real. I could see it cross Pip's face. They had hoped for a draw, something that would make the square full and the donation box heavier.\n\nMaui noticed.\n\n\"I can help with something else,\" he offered. \"If you need it.\"\n\nPip looked toward the pile of lantern hooks.\n\n\"Actually...\"\n\nWe spent the next two days preparing the celebration.\n\nOn the first afternoon, I found Maui behind the stack of stage screens arguing with a draft program. He'd drawn himself three times larger than the performers.\n\n\"It's marketing,\" he said. \"You lead with your strongest feature.\"\n\n\"The strongest feature is a festival everybody can attend.\"\n\n\"Second strongest.\"\n\nEdda came past with the approved route plan. The space beside the ramp needed to stay open even if a crowd moved suddenly toward the lantern display. Maui offered to stand there and direct everybody.\n\n\"You're the thing they'd rush toward,\" Edda said.\n\nHe looked delighted, which proved her point.\n\n\"Could you carry these spare screens into the west storage room?\" she asked. \"Without announcing it from the stage?\"\n\n\"The cruelty here is astounding.\"\n\nHe carried them, although he made a show of tiptoeing under their weight. At the doorway he tried to salute, bumped his shoulder against the lintel and glared at the building as though it had challenged him personally.\n\nEdda checked the cleared turning area. \"Better.\"\n\n\"Remember this day,\" Maui told me. \"I saved a festival with a doorway.\"\n\n\"I'll have it engraved on something tiny.\"\n\n\"Nothing tiny could contain the legend.\"\n\nHe kept the ridiculous draft program folded in his shirt. I didn't ask him to throw it away. Pip's real program could still belong to everybody else.\n\nGreg would play a short original set, because he had chosen to and could leave when it was over. The children from Tidewake wanted to perform their little story about Fen falling into the harbor. Fen protested until they discovered they had been given the role of the wise crab.\n\nIroh offered tea. Mara brought bread. Tamsin made sure the decorations were mounted on independent supports rather than the ancient arches. Edda checked the viewing areas so people could sit or stand without blocking the routes.\n\nMaui helped Pip sort hooks.\n\nIt was possibly the least glamorous work he'd ever done.\n\nHe was very bad at sorting.\n\n\"Those are the long ones,\" Pip told him.\n\n\"They look short.\"\n\n\"Compared with your hand, everything looks short.\"\n\nI laughed from the next table.\n\nMaui held up a hook.\n\n\"This one?\"\n\n\"Medium.\"\n\nHe sighed.\n\n\"How can there be so many kinds of hook?\"\n\n\"How can there be so many kinds of fish?\" Pip asked.\n\nHe considered.\n\n\"Excellent point.\"\n\nOn the evening before the festival, the volunteers met to confirm what the celebration actually required. Pip had written a cheerful program, but the schedule had no break between the children's kite display and the lantern procession. Greg was listed for three songs despite having agreed to two.\n\n\"He'll still do three,\" somebody said. \"He likes playing.\"\n\n\"Ask him,\" I said.\n\nWe found Greg sorting cables by the stage. He was happy to play two. The third song wasn't ready, and he'd promised to be back in Beach City the next morning. Nobody was entitled to turn a friendly offer into an obligation.\n\nPip revised the program without grumbling. The children chose a shorter kite demonstration because Fen had posted an afternoon wind warning. Tamsin checked that the lantern stands could be moved without blocking Edda's turning space. Mara counted the bread orders against the bakery's actual flour supply.\n\nMaui carried chairs while we worked. When one volunteer asked whether he could still give a short heroic introduction, he stopped and looked at the children waiting to test their lantern hooks.\n\n\"I'd rather introduce the people who did the repairs,\" he said. \"If they want to speak.\"\n\nSeveral of them didn't. The council decided a name could go on the public thanks list only with permission; private volunteers could be thanked collectively. One worker asked not to be identified at all. Pip crossed the name out without asking why.\n\nLater Maui admitted that he'd imagined the crowd chanting for him.\n\n\"You can want applause,\" I said. \"You just can't collect it on someone else's behalf.\"\n\n\"I know. I just really wanted them to chant my name. In rhythm. Then I felt like an idiot for wanting it.\"\n\n\"It doesn't. You look ridiculous when you try to hide behind a basket of lantern hooks.\"\n\nHe peered over the basket. \"Then my disguise has failed.\"\n\nI laughed and kissed him on the cheek. There was no grand lesson written on the program, only accurate times, safe paths, and people who'd agreed to be there.\n\nPip brought the revised poster round after sunset, its corners curling where the ink was still wet.\n\n\"Could you look before I hang it?\" they asked Maui.\n\nHe held it at arm's length. The words were smaller than before. The children's performance sat at the top, followed by the inspection-fund supper and the lantern walk. No one's name occupied half the paper.\n\nMaui swallowed.\n\n\"Are you disappointed?\" Pip asked.\n\nHe took much too long to answer. \"A little.\"\n\nPip's mouth tightened. I thought they might apologize, though they hadn't done anything wrong.\n\n\"I used to like seeing my name,\" Maui said. \"That isn't your responsibility.\"\n\n\"It's okay to have a name on the poster,\" Pip replied. \"You were helping with the benches.\"\n\nHe looked down at the volunteer list, where his name stood between Lio and Edda. \"It seems very small.\"\n\n\"So is mine,\" Pip said. \"I painted six of them.\"\n\nThat made him laugh. Pip did not join in until he stopped. Then they asked whether he would carry the sign to the square, because the paint was wet and he had steady hands.\n\nI watched him take it carefully by the wooden frame instead of holding it over his head like a victory banner.\n\nLater, while we walked through the lantern-lit market, Maui caught my sleeve.\n\n\"I know you love other people,\" he said. \"I know I'm not supposed to turn this into a competition. But I miss being somebody everyone looks for.\"\n\n\"Do you want me to pretend I don't see you?\"\n\n\"No.\" He rubbed the back of his neck. \"I want you to notice the parts nobody else looks for.\"\n\nI thought of the way he'd carried the poster so Pip's paint wouldn't smudge. Of his quiet face when a child asked a question he couldn't answer. I told him both.\n\nHis smile arrived slowly, uncertain at the edges.\n\n\"That feels different,\" he said.\n\n\"It is.\"\n\nHe leaned close, then hesitated because people were walking past with armfuls of chairs. I pulled him aside by the lantern shed and kissed him, deliberately and a little awkwardly, with the smell of wet paint still on his hands. We laughed when a hook rattled above us. He didn't ask whether anyone had seen.\n\nBy festival morning, the poster had been replaced. It listed the schedule without turning any person into the center of the world. Maui's name appeared under *volunteer setup*, alongside Pip, Lio, Edda, and a dozen others.\n\nHe looked at it for a long moment.\n\n\"Do you regret it?\" I asked.\n\n\"Some of it.\"\n\nI nodded.\n\n\"Which part?\"\n\n\"I like being seen.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\n\"I think I always will.\"\n\n\"That's not a crime.\"\n\nHe looked relieved.\n\n\"I just don't want to need it to know who I am.\"\n\nWe stood together beneath the lanterns.\n\nThen Lio shouted for help with the stage ramp, and Maui went to ask what they needed.\n\nThe celebration began late because the first tray of bread had to be rescued from an overenthusiastic oven. Nobody minded. Aang helped the children launch a few kites from the safe ridge, keeping sacred Air Nomad traditions separate from a local craft festival. Toph called the whole event \"terribly sentimental\" and then refused to leave before the last performance.\n\nGreg played a song about a ferry bell.\n\nIt was quieter than the crowd expected.\n\nPeople listened.\n\nI saw Maui standing at the edge of the square, holding a spare lantern pole in one hand. He wasn't watching the stage. He was watching a child who couldn't quite reach the hook they'd been assigned.\n\nHe knelt, held the pole steady, and asked whether they wanted help.\n\nThe child nodded.\n\nTogether they hung the lantern.\n\nNo applause.\n\nNo one even turned to look.\n\nSomething in my chest ached.\n\nLater, when the crowd began moving toward the food stalls, Maui came to find me.\n\n\"Did I miss anything?\"\n\n\"The wise crab was excellent.\"\n\n\"Naturally.\"\n\n\"Greg played beautifully.\"\n\n\"I heard.\"\n\n\"And you helped Pip.\"\n\nHe shrugged.\n\nI stepped closer.\n\n\"I saw you with the lantern.\"\n\nHis eyes widened.\n\n\"You did?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe looked almost shy.\n\n\"Was it all right?\"\n\n\"It was lovely.\"\n\nHe let out a breath.\n\nI took his hand and pulled him behind the sheltered garden wall, out of the flow of people. The lanterns made soft gold patterns on the stone.\n\n\"Can I kiss you?\" I asked.\n\nHis smile was sudden and warm.\n\n\"Please.\"\n\nI kissed him. His hands settled carefully at my waist, and for a few moments the festival noise became a distant, pleasant blur. We laughed against each other when a paper fish caught in his hair.\n\n\"Your audience has arrived,\" I whispered.\n\n\"A terrible critic.\"\n\n\"Very.\"\n\nWe rejoined the crowd after the lantern keeper checked the final hooks. The donation box held enough to fund part of the next inspection, and the public ledger recorded the amount. No one promised the service tunnel would reopen because the celebration had gone well.\n\nAt the end, Pip thanked the volunteers.\n\nMaui was named with everyone else.\n\nHe clapped for the children.\n\nWhen we walked home, he carried no trophy.\n\nOnly a small bent hook he'd rescued from the sorting pile, because he thought we could use it in the garden.\n\nWe couldn't.\n\nI let him keep it anyway.","word_count":2416},"maui-06":{"number":6,"title":"A Song for the Unfinished","subtitle":"The Shore I Choose · Chapter 6 of 10","content":"The first visitors from Aotearoa reached the Lanternweald on an evening of mist.\n\nTheir crates arrived on a different cart. One was marked with the name of the person responsible for the instruments; another carried tools for repairing a loose frame before anything was performed. The visitor at the back checked each strap before the load was lifted.\n\nMaui eyed the biggest box. “I could carry the entire thing.”\n\nMereana stepped between him and the handle.\n\n“I know you could. Do you know how it's packed?”\n\n“Carefully?”\n\n“Not an answer.”\n\nMini Maui struck a grand pose on his shoulder, then pretended to be crushed by an invisible crate. I had to look away to keep from laughing.\n\nMereana asked her own team to unload the instruments and gave Maui a job they had agreed to: stand at the far end of the path and keep the crowd clear while the fragile case was moved. He looked genuinely disappointed for perhaps three seconds.\n\n“Very well,” he announced. “The great Maui shall guard the path against reckless admirers.”\n\n“Wonderful,” I said. “Try not to start a fan club.”\n\nHe winked. But he moved the crowd, and nobody had to hand over an object they knew how to carry to a stranger who wanted to demonstrate his strength.\n\nThe gathering felt different after that. These visitors had chosen what to bring and what to share, and I wanted the valley to be the sort of place that remembered those choices.\n\nThey came through a Threadway carrying instruments, wrapped food, and stories they had chosen to share. Their organizer, Mereana, introduced herself as a Māori performer and teacher. She spoke carefully about her community, her own teachers, and the fact that no single person could speak for every iwi or every tradition.\n\nMaui tried to greet her with the sort of theatrical flourish he usually reserved for a crowd.\n\nMereana smiled. “I have met impressive storytellers before.”\n\n“Impressive? Sure. But has anybody here persuaded the sun to change its schedule? No? Then I have a truly magnificent introduction prepared.”\n\n“Then perhaps you'll enjoy listening for a change.”\n\nI had to turn away to hide my grin.\n\nThe gathering took place beneath a roof of woven branches beside Lanternwell Square. Mereana spoke of the whare tapere, places associated with storytelling, recreation, music, movement, and meetings between people, including lovers in some traditions. Her group offered a contemporary presentation: a poi performance shaped around a story of separation and reunion, paired with a waiata aroha they had chosen for the evening.\n\nShe explained that waiata aroha could carry longing, grief, or love; poi had its own histories and purposes; and haka was not a generic courtship routine. This evening's piece was their artistic interpretation, not a claim to perform an ancient universal wedding ritual.\n\nI listened to the rhythm of the poi and the steadiness of the performers. The motions were practiced, the story held in a community, not borrowed for someone else's decoration.\n\nBeside me Maui went quiet.\n\nAfterward, Mereana invited questions. Maui asked about the history of the performance with a seriousness that surprised me. He did not pretend that all Polynesian islands shared one identical custom. He listened when she spoke of Aotearoa and of the traditions particular to her people.\n\nMereana listened to Maui's question before answering. He had asked whether a song could be shared outside the gathering without making it belong to the person who carried it away.\n\n\"Some songs are offered for people to join,\" she said. \"Some aren't. You ask the people responsible, and you listen to the answer.\"\n\nMaui nodded with an expression that suggested he had just been given a challenge worthy of a demigod.\n\n\"Excellent. I am legendary at asking.\"\n\n\"That seems doubtful,\" I said.\n\nMini Maui appeared near his shoulder and shook his tiny head so vigorously I nearly laughed aloud.\n\n\"I can improve,\" Maui announced. \"My second-greatest talent, after being magnificent.\"\n\nMereana smiled, but she did not give him the performance to embellish. She introduced the people who had prepared the instruments, including the person who would decide how they were carried home. Maui offered to help with a crate when they finished. He was told where to take it and, for once, didn't make a speech about how easy it was to lift.\n\nI walked beside him across the damp stones.\n\n\"Are you disappointed?\" I asked.\n\n\"What, that nobody asked me to sing my own praises?\"\n\n\"Among other things.\"\n\n\"Little bit.\" He looked toward the performers, then back at me. \"They were good. Better than my speech would've been.\"\n\n\"Those words must have hurt.\"\n\n\"Terribly.\"\n\nHis hand found mine, but he waited for my fingers to close around it. The music remained behind us, where its makers could choose what happened next. I could still feel the rhythm in my feet without pretending I'd earned any part of it.\n\nMaui's little tattoo raised both arms for an encore that had not been requested. He pressed a broad finger against it, as if that would keep his own enthusiasm from bursting out.\n\nMereana noticed. \"Is that part of your act?\"\n\n\"Only the extremely talented part.\" He grinned. \"The rest of the act is more handsome.\"\n\nShe laughed once. \"We're not doing requests tonight.\"\n\n\"Well, there goes my plan to make a spectacular entrance.\"\n\n\"You're already here.\"\n\n\"Exactly. Wasted opportunity.\"\n\nHe stepped back when her ensemble began packing their own equipment. I could see how hard it was for him not to offer a flourish or take over the carrying. When Mereana asked for help with one marked case, though, he hoisted it as if it were the most valuable treasure on the island and took it exactly where she pointed. He was still Maui; he just hadn't mistaken somebody else's stage for his.\n\nWhen the visitors began serving food, Maui drew me aside toward the edge of the square.\n\n“I had a big speech planned,” he admitted.\n\n“That's shocking.”\n\n“I know. But then I realized it was their night to tell a story.”\n\n“That's almost growth.”\n\nHe put a hand over his heart. “You wound me.”\n\nWe walked between the lanterns while distant voices carried from the gathering. Maui looked at his tattoos.\n\n“Every mark says what I did,” he said. “People think that means they know everything about me.”\n\nI thought of the name I'd found in the spring, and all the rest still hidden.\n\n“Maybe it only means they know where some stories begin.”\n\nHe considered that.\n\nThen he held out his hand.\n\n“Dance?”\n\n“Did you learn that from Mereana?”\n\n“No. I'm asking you to dance with me. Our own steps. No pretending it's somebody else's tradition.”\n\nI took his hand.\n\nHe tried an exaggerated turn that would have looked heroic if he hadn't nearly stepped into a flower bed. I laughed, pulled him back, and found a gentler rhythm on the lanternlit stones.\n\nThere was nothing ceremonial about it. It was clumsy, private in the middle of a public square, and entirely ours.\n\nMaui watched my face as though waiting for me to decide whether I wanted to keep going.\n\nI did.\n\nThe voices from the gathering rose warmly behind us. The people who had shared their art remained its owners. I carried away an understanding, not a souvenir.\n\nAnd in the soft circle of lanternlight, I chose another dance.\n\nOn the way back from the gathering, I stopped beside the little bridge that led to the bakery. One of the paper lanterns had come loose and was turning in the breeze, tapping its wooden frame against a post.\n\nMaui caught it before it fell.\n\n\"Another heroic rescue,\" he announced. \"You may applaud.\"\n\n\"Careful. You'll encourage yourself.\"\n\n\"Too late.\"\n\nHe hooked the lantern back into place. His hands were broad enough to make the little brass catch look ridiculous. I watched him test it twice before letting go.\n\n\"What?\" he asked.\n\n\"Nothing. I just like that you checked.\"\n\nHe grinned. \"I've been known to take things seriously.\"\n\nBefore I began, I watched Mereana speak with the younger performers while they packed their equipment. One of them asked whether the visitors could repeat a verse outside the planned gathering.\n\n\"We can discuss it with everyone who performed,\" Mereana answered. \"A song offered tonight isn't an invitation for somebody else to decide where we sing tomorrow.\"\n\nMaui went very still beside me.\n\n\"That's fair,\" he said.\n\n\"I thought you'd want an encore.\"\n\n\"I did. But I didn't ask.\"\n\nMereana met his eyes and gave him a small smile. \"You can enjoy what you heard without owning the next performance.\"\n\nI was grateful she'd said it. The evening had been beautiful, and it would have been easy to turn her community's art into scenery for our own relationship.\n\nLater, after the gathering, Maui found me sitting on the low wall by the lanterns. He sat down with enough space between us that it was unmistakably a choice.\n\n\"There's something I don't know how to ask,\" he admitted.\n\n\"Try badly first.\"\n\nHe snorted. \"A very generous invitation.\"\n\n\"I have experience with bad first drafts.\"\n\nHe looked at the lantern casting light across the stones. \"If I danced with you, would you think I was asking for more than a dance?\"\n\n\"I'd ask what you meant.\"\n\n\"Which would ruin my mysterious charm.\"\n\n\"Your mysterious charm has never survived five minutes.\"\n\nHe laughed, then sobered. \"I can take an answer that isn't the one I hoped for.\"\n\n\"I hope so. I don't want anybody guessing a promise from a hand held or a song heard.\"\n\nHe nodded. When I told him I wanted to speak openly about how I loved, he didn't interrupt. For once the lanterns and the music were enough of a stage, and neither of us needed the audience to stay.\n\n\"I know.\" I took a breath. \"There's something I want to tell you while we're still figuring out what this is.\"\n\nHis grin wavered. \"That sounds suspiciously like a speech.\"\n\n\"It might be. Try not to interrupt the important bits.\"\n\n\"I'll be magnificent at listening.\"\n\n\"I've never really wanted an exclusive relationship. I'm ethically non-monogamous. I prefer polyamory.\"\n\nHe lowered the arm he'd been preparing to flourish with.\n\nI went on before courage could become an excuse to retreat. \"I mean honesty. People knowing what they're agreeing to. No sneaking around, no promising someone they're the only person I'll ever love and then hoping they never ask questions. Love doesn't feel smaller to me because it has room for more than one person.\"\n\nMaui was quiet long enough that the tide reached the rocks below us and slid back again.\n\n\"More than one person,\" he repeated.\n\n\"Potentially. I'm not asking you to meet anyone. I'm not saying there is somebody else right now. I am saying I won't pretend to want exclusivity because I like you.\"\n\nHe looked at the lantern, then at me. \"So the part where I show up and make an idiot of myself doesn't give me the right to decide the rest of your life?\"\n\n\"That part is mostly decorative.\"\n\n\"I suspected.\" His smile returned, smaller this time. \"I don't know all the rules.\"\n\n\"Neither do I. We'd have to make agreements together. You could be jealous. I could be jealous. We'd talk instead of treating feelings like crimes.\"\n\nHe sat on the bridge rail, giving me room beside him. \"I've spent a long time wanting people to pick me. The best. The strongest. The only hero.\"\n\n\"And?\"\n\n\"And I think I'd like to find out who I am when someone chooses me without making a trophy out of it.\" He rubbed the back of his neck. \"I'd want honesty, Jasper. Especially when it makes me nervous. And I might need to ask some truly stupid questions.\"\n\n\"I can survive those.\"\n\n\"Bold claim.\"\n\nI took the seat beside him. Our shoulders did not quite touch. Neither of us moved away.\n\n\"Thank you for telling me before I tried to impress you with another dance,\" he said.\n\n\"You are going to do that anyway.\"\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\nHe offered his hand, palm open, without taking mine. I set my fingers in it because I wanted to.\n\nWe stayed there until the lantern stopped rattling.","word_count":2043},"maui-20":{"number":20,"title":"The Second Bell","subtitle":"The Lanternweald — fourth expansion pass","content":"The second bell sounded beneath the sea just after the Lanternwell rang noon.\n\nI knew the first sound. Everyone did. It was the old brass bell above the spring, low and clear, used to mark meetings and emergency route changes. Its note traveled over the square and down the canal.\n\nThe second note came a heartbeat later.\n\nIt was deeper. Not louder, exactly. I felt it in my chest before I understood it was a sound.\n\nThe water in the cove shivered.\n\nMaui stopped halfway through lifting a crate.\n\n\"All right, I know I'm famous for pulling impossible things out of the ocean, but that?\" Maui said, the crate still balanced in his arms. \"That was not me.\"\n\n\"I hadn't accused you.\"\n\n\"You were thinking it.\"\n\nI wasn't. Not until he said it.\n\nFen emerged from the storage shed carrying the tide chart. The line of their mouth had gone flat.\n\n\"Inside,\" they said.\n\n\"Why?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"Because the tide's turning and I want to hear the next sound without everyone shouting.\"\n\nWe followed them into the shelter beneath the old signal post. Neri arrived soon afterward, breathing harder than usual. Tamsin came with a measuring rod. Danse brought a small instrument for recording vibration. Severus, who had been examining the damaged ledger fragment at Moonroot, carried a copy of the old diagram.\n\nIt was the first time I'd seen those people gather without somebody needing medical help.\n\nI did not find it reassuring.\n\n\"Did the bell ring twice?\" Neri asked.\n\n\"Yes,\" I said. \"The second sound came from below the landing.\"\n\nNeri closed their eyes.\n\nMaui looked at them.\n\n\"You knew this could happen.\"\n\n\"I knew it happened before.\"\n\n\"When?\"\n\n\"Years ago.\"\n\n\"How many?\"\n\nNeri looked away.\n\nI felt anger rise, but I forced myself to listen. We had already learned that shame could make people guard information long past the point where it helped anyone.\n\n\"Tell us what matters for safety first,\" I said.\n\nNeri nodded, slowly.\n\n\"The old service route runs beneath the spring and follows the water toward Tideglass. It was used to drain the floodplain during severe storms. It has been sealed for generations.\"\n\n\"We know about the route,\" I said. \"We need to know whether it's moving water now.\"\n\nThey looked at the tide chart.\n\n\"If it is, the seal may be under pressure.\"\n\nDanse set his instrument on the workbench.\n\n\"The second bell could be resonance. Moving water can make a cavity sound if there's an opening with the right shape.\"\n\nSeverus studied the copied diagram.\n\n\"Or a change in pressure could be moving an old metal fitting. The documents mention a warning device.\"\n\nMaui folded his arms.\n\n\"Oh, come on,\" he said. \"One mysterious bell beneath the sea. That's the beginning of a great story. Perhaps a lost kingdom! An ancient beast! A treasure so glorious it requires a demigod with excellent hair!\"\n\nMini Maui acted out all three possibilities on his chest. For the ancient beast, the tiny figure used both arms to make an absurd set of jaws.\n\nSeverus looked deeply unimpressed. Danse looked as though he wanted to inventory the imaginary jaws.\n\n\"I'll get an actual measurement first,\" Fen said.\n\nMaui blinked. \"You people know how to ruin suspense.\"\n\n\"I don't mind suspense,\" I said. \"I mind getting washed out to sea by it.\"\n\nHis grin became crooked. The hook was already in his hand; he wanted the adventure so badly I could almost feel it tugging him toward the stone.\n\n\"Fine,\" he said. \"We solve the riddle the boring way. Then—when it's safe—you let me name the ridiculous thing.\"\n\n\"Absolutely not,\" Tamsin said.\n\n\"See? No respect for greatness.\"\n\nHe stepped back from the lower passage without pretending he'd stopped wanting to open it. That was the difficult part of the choice.\n\n\"So there's a bell under the water.\"\n\n\"Possibly,\" Danse said.\n\n\"That's what I said.\"\n\n\"Possibly is a useful word,\" I told him.\n\nWe decided on measurements before theories. Fen recorded the height of the tide. Tamsin marked the waterline against the landing stones. Danse set up his instrument where the old diagram suggested a service opening might lie. Severus compared the copied ledger with the notes Neri had brought.\n\nNobody went below the surface.\n\nMaui was unhappy about that.\n\n\"There's a tunnel,\" he said. \"I can go see it.\"\n\n\"And if it's unstable?\" Tamsin asked.\n\n\"I can get out.\"\n\n\"And if your movement collapses the channel and sends water into Sela's garden?\"\n\nHe stopped.\n\nIt was an uncomfortable victory for the last several months of learning to ask.\n\nWe waited through the next hour.\n\nThe sea did not ring again.\n\nBy late afternoon the tide had fallen enough to reveal a pattern of stones beyond the repaired mooring. They formed a shallow arc. I had walked past them dozens of times. I thought they were part of the old breakwater.\n\nFen crouched beside them.\n\n\"That's not a breakwater,\" they said.\n\nFen marked three points along the exposed stones with bits of colored cord. None was a sign that the passage could be opened.\n\n\"Here's what we actually know,\" they said. \"The stones are older than the new mooring. They shifted in the last surge. And the sound reached us when the surface bell rang.\"\n\nMaui crouched beside the nearest one. \"And here's what I know. Water's been moving through that hollow for longer than the dock's been here.\"\n\n\"You can tell?\"\n\n\"Look at the worn edge. The current's done that, not one bad storm.\"\n\nFen ran a finger along the stone and nodded. \"Good observation. Still need a measurement.\"\n\nHe made a face. \"You people and your measurements.\"\n\n\"You people and your habit of lifting things before asking.\"\n\n\"An unfairly accurate summary.\"\n\nI wrote both observations in separate lines. Neri could compare them with the archive once the records were available. We were not going to call a pattern of stones a door, and we were certainly not going to call an echo a person.\n\nMaui tilted his head at the sea. Mini Maui pressed a drawn ear to his own tiny chest, then shrugged.\n\n\"I could go down there as a fish,\" he offered.\n\n\"And get caught where we can't reach you,\" Fen replied. \"No.\"\n\nHis jaw tightened. I recognized the struggle between wanting to help and wanting to be the only one who could. Then he stood and offered to carry the measuring pole back to the dry path.\n\n\"Fine. The great Maui, champion of boring equipment.\"\n\n\"You're very good at it,\" I said.\n\n\"Don't tell anyone. It'll ruin my legend.\"\n\nThe water was already covering the first mark. We left before the tide made the decision for us.\n\n\"Then what is it?\"\n\n\"A lip for an overflow channel.\"\n\nTamsin measured the gap between the stones.\n\n\"Someone built this to let water out without washing the landing away.\"\n\n\"Did it work?\" I asked.\n\nNeri stood behind us.\n\n\"Sometimes.\"\n\nThe word was so small it almost vanished in the wind.\n\nI turned.\n\n\"What happened the other times?\"\n\nThey looked toward the village.\n\n\"During the Closed Bells, the keepers used the service route to manage the flooding. They also used the same doors and locks to control who could leave.\"\n\nMaui's hand moved toward his hook.\n\nI touched his wrist.\n\nNeri continued.\n\n\"The people who made the decision believed they were preventing deaths. Some people were frightened enough to agree. Others wanted to go home and were told to wait.\"\n\n\"Who sealed the passage?\" I asked.\n\nNeri looked at the wet stones.\n\n\"That is part of the history I haven't finished telling.\"\n\n\"Because you're ashamed?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Because someone living might be hurt by it?\"\n\nThey nodded.\n\nI wanted to shake an answer loose. I wanted the satisfaction of making the old silence end with one brave question.\n\nInstead I thought of Lark leaving through the eastern arch, and of the old register's expected-return column.\n\n\"We can't force a confession,\" I said. \"But the structural danger isn't private.\"\n\nNeri met my eyes.\n\n\"No. It isn't.\"\n\nWe agreed to post a public notice about the possible pressure change and schedule an engineering inspection. The route near the old stones would close temporarily. The cove would remain accessible by the safe path around the upper ridge. Fen would check the tide twice a day until the next survey.\n\nThat night, people gathered around the notice board.\n\nSome were angry. Some were frightened. A few insisted the second bell was a spirit calling them to the sea. Others wanted Maui to tear open the stone immediately and settle the matter with strength.\n\nHe looked at me.\n\nI did not answer for him.\n\nHe stepped forward.\n\n\"I've fought things that wanted to kill me,\" he said. \"I've made bigger mistakes because I was too sure I knew what the danger was. We don't know what's under there yet. I'm not breaking a wall while people live behind it.\"\n\nThe square went quiet.\n\nI felt a strange pride that had nothing to do with his reputation.\n\nAfter the meeting, I found Neri at Moonroot Archive. The old ledger lay between two sheets of protective cloth.\n\n\"Why the seventh scratch?\" I asked.\n\nNeri hesitated.\n\n\"Six public paths and one service passage. The mark wasn't a door for travelers. It was a way for maintenance crews to know where the old water route crossed the foundation.\"\n\n\"And my key?\"\n\n\"Shared pattern. The teeth were cut for maintenance hatches, not for ownership of the vale.\"\n\nI touched the brass key at my throat.\n\nI wasn't a chosen keeper. I wasn't the inheritor of a magical empire. For a moment that disappointed the part of me that wanted my missing past to mean something grand.\n\nThen I felt relief.\n\n\"So I can choose what it means now,\" I said.\n\nNeri looked at me.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nOutside, the lanterns were being lit along the safe path. People had already begun arguing about the inspection costs. It was a reassuringly ordinary sound.\n\nMaui met me beside the fountain.\n\n\"Any answers?\"\n\n\"Some.\"\n\n\"Enough?\"\n\n\"Not yet.\"\n\nHe took my hand.\n\nWe walked home past the cove. The tide was rising. Beneath it the stones waited, not a prophecy, not a reward, but a problem our neighbors would have to solve together.\n\nThe bell did not ring again that night.\n\nI didn't mistake the silence for safety.","word_count":1724},"maui-33":{"number":33,"title":"The Unfinished Song","subtitle":"Lanternweald — sixth expansion pass","content":"Greg had written four lines and crossed out three.\n\nHe showed me the page without pretending it was finished.\n\n\"Something's missing,\" he said.\n\nThe Starwake Pier stage was empty that morning. The western boards had reopened after inspection, but the public performance area wasn't booked until the weekend. A breeze rattled the spare chairs in the storage shed. Greg had brought his repaired guitar; the scratch along the body still caught the light.\n\nMaui followed us down the pier carrying a basket of breakfast rolls.\n\n\"Great!\" he said when Greg explained the problem. \"I'll give you a verse about the sea. Storms, great deeds, an enormous hero—\"\n\n\"I was hoping for something smaller,\" Greg said.\n\n\"Small hero? Easy. You should have seen me as a beetle. Most impressive beetle in recorded history. Six legs, unbelievable presence.\"\n\n\"No hero, actually.\"\n\nMaui looked personally wounded.\n\nMini Maui appeared on his chest, threw up both arms as if Greg had insulted the entire history of heroes, and pointed toward the sea for dramatic effect.\n\nMaui gave the tattoo an appreciative nod. \"Exactly! You ask the demigod with the greatest maritime résumé ever assembled for a song and then say you don't need the hero part?\"\n\nGreg plucked one note. \"I asked you to hang out, man.\"\n\n\"Which I am also excellent at. Some would say legendary.\"\n\n\"You can be legendary at not interrupting for a minute.\"\n\nMaui put a finger to his lips. The tattoo mimed him with such exaggerated innocence that I knew silence wouldn't last long. I caught Greg smiling into the guitar. There was something affectionate in how he let Maui be ridiculous without rewarding the bid to make every conversation about his deeds.\n\nA gull cried above the rail. Maui looked up, briefly considering a bird transformation.\n\n\"Don't,\" I whispered.\n\n\"I wasn't.\"\n\n\"You absolutely were.\"\n\nHe flashed a grin. Greg found the next note without either of us needing to explain why the silence had mattered.\n\nGreg played the four-note phrase again, the one he'd built from the ferry bell at Tideglass. The fifth note rose and settled in a way that reminded me of somebody returning after a long absence.\n\n\"See?\" he said. \"After that it needs a breath, but whenever I write words, I fill it up.\"\n\nMaui leaned on the railing. \"The problem is you're thinking too quietly. The ocean—\"\n\n\"Can you just sit with me for a while?\" Greg interrupted.\n\nThere was nothing angry in his voice. He looked tired.\n\nMaui stared. \"You don't need me to do anything?\"\n\n\"I asked because I like having you around. You're good at looking at the water without getting bored.\"\n\nMaui sat down beside Greg with such exaggerated care that the bench creaked in complaint.\n\n\"See? Not doing anything. A triumph of restraint.\"\n\nGreg plucked one quiet note from the guitar.\n\n\"You're doing great, man.\"\n\n\"Normally I would have saved at least three boats by now.\"\n\n\"Sure. But then you'd miss the hard part.\"\n\n\"Which is?\"\n\n\"Waiting until the song knows where it wants to go.\"\n\nMaui squinted. \"Songs don't know things.\"\n\n\"Neither does the sea, technically, but you've spent a lot of time listening to it.\"\n\nI saw the moment that landed. Maui looked out toward the tide. Greg kept one hand on the strings but didn't play, giving him room to think without setting a trap.\n\n\"My hook can turn me into a whole menagerie,\" Maui said eventually. \"Not once has it made me a better listener.\"\n\n\"Could turn into a bird,\" Greg suggested. \"Big ears. Terrible singing voice.\"\n\n\"That's not how birds work.\"\n\n\"You'd know. You're the expert.\"\n\nMaui threw his head back and laughed. His confidence hadn't disappeared. It just had somewhere to rest that wasn't an audience.\n\nGreg tried the four-note phrase again. Maui kept the rhythm with one enormous finger against his knee and asked before adding a second beat. It was a small thing, but I noticed: he had stopped treating Greg's unfinished music as an empty space waiting for his own heroism.\n\nWhen I leaned against Maui, he lowered his hand from the rail and hooked a finger gently around mine. Greg let the last note hang. None of us rushed to fill it.\n\n\"I'm also good at a lot of other things.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nThe silence that followed made me want to jump in and explain both men to each other. Maui was trying to contribute; Greg wanted company. They could probably solve it themselves.\n\nSo I carried the breakfast basket to the bench and passed out the rolls.\n\nGreg played again.\n\nMaui lasted almost two minutes without speaking. Then he pointed toward the far end of the pier.\n\n\"That board is loose.\"\n\n\"It passed inspection,\" I said.\n\n\"It's making a sound.\"\n\nGreg stopped playing. \"What kind of sound?\"\n\nMaui tapped the railing once, listening. \"Like the line on a boat when it starts taking weight. Not danger exactly. Just something asking to be checked.\"\n\nGreg looked at his guitar. \"Huh.\"\n\nI knew the look. He'd found a thought he wanted to keep.\n\n\"Don't make him sing about the board,\" I warned.\n\nGreg grinned. \"I might.\"\n\nMaui looked pleased. \"Finally. A subject worthy of a song.\"\n\nThe three of us sat until the wind shifted. We listened to ropes clicking against cleats and water moving below the supports. The low note from the old drainage passage did not sound; Fen had recorded an ordinary tide that morning. There was no mystery demanding we stop enjoying breakfast.\n\nAfter a while Greg asked Maui, \"What's the sound you miss most from home?\"\n\nMaui didn't answer immediately.\n\nThen he said, \"The moment just before a wave breaks on the reef. People hear the crash. I hear the water pulling itself together.\"\n\nGreg nodded and played a note without words.\n\nMaui listened.\n\n\"That one,\" he said quietly.\n\nGreg tried it again. He moved two fingers along the strings, leaving a longer gap after the fifth note.\n\nI could see Maui wanting to fill it. He opened his mouth, closed it, and looked down at his hands.\n\n\"Do you want me to do something?\" he finally asked.\n\n\"Stay,\" Greg said.\n\nMaui nodded.\n\nThe answer was almost painfully simple.\n\nBefore Steven came, Tamsin walked down the safe side of the pier with a clipboard tucked beneath her arm. She heard the same faint rattle Maui had pointed out and stopped.\n\n\"Good ear,\" she told him. \"It passed the earlier check, but this wind is different. I'll log it before the next set.\"\n\nMaui looked so pleased that Greg grinned.\n\n\"See? You helped without writing me a sea-king anthem.\"\n\n\"I still have excellent rhymes available.\"\n\n\"Keep them for later.\"\n\nTamsin tested the cleat and marked the board for a second inspection before the weekend performance. It wasn't an emergency, and she didn't need the whole stage closed. The tiny distinction mattered: noticing a problem wasn't the same as declaring everything ruined, and somebody qualified had to decide what the sound meant.\n\nGreg stopped playing until Tamsin finished. He asked whether the inspection would change his rehearsal time and offered to move to the sheltered east bench if it did. She said that would be sensible, thanked him, and went back to her work.\n\nNola came up the pier with a coil of yellow rope over one shoulder. The knot in it was so complicated I couldn't tell whether it had been tied on purpose.\n\n\"You're moving?\" she asked Greg.\n\n\"Temporarily. Tamsin's got a second look at the cleat.\"\n\n\"Good. I need the east bench for the afternoon instrument return. If you can finish before then, you're welcome to use it.\"\n\nGreg checked his watch. \"Thirty minutes?\"\n\n\"Twenty-five.\"\n\nHe made a face. \"You're a tough booking agent.\"\n\n\"I'm the one who puts the chairs away when the booking agent goes home.\"\n\nMaui reached for her rope. \"I can carry that.\"\n\n\"You can ask where it goes,\" Nola said, passing it to him.\n\nI hid my smile behind a breakfast roll. He'd been about to offer to carry every rope on the pier as though strength were the only thing the place lacked.\n\nGreg sat on the steps with his notebook balanced on his knee. The unfinished song had room for another line, but his pencil hovered over the page.\n\n\"What if the missing part isn't words?\" Maui asked.\n\nGreg looked up. \"Go on.\"\n\nMaui glanced at the loose cleat, the empty stage, and Nola dragging a chair into storage. \"You stop playing when somebody needs to say something. Maybe the song needs somewhere to stop.\"\n\nGreg stared at him.\n\n\"That is either beautiful or incredibly annoying,\" he said.\n\n\"Both,\" I offered.\n\nHe laughed, then struck a chord and let it fade without filling the silence. The ropes creaked softly, and somewhere underneath the boards a gull complained about the morning.\n\n\"Yeah,\" Greg said. \"That's closer.\"\n\nMaui was grinning so hard it almost hurt to look at him. He had helped make music without demanding that anybody sing about him. Nola called from the shed to remind us that twenty-five minutes did not expand just because the tune was improving.\n\nGreg closed his notebook and picked up the guitar. He had a deadline. The unfinished line could wait for him.\n\nWe carried the guitar case to the bench. Maui looked at the cleared stage.\n\n\"I thought the sound was only something to fix.\"\n\n\"Sometimes it's a piece of a song,\" Greg said. \"Sometimes it's a safety problem. You can listen for both.\"\n\nMaui considered that, then leaned against the railing with his breakfast roll.\n\nI wanted to say something important about the way he was learning. Instead I asked if he wanted the last piece of bread.\n\n\"Obviously.\"\n\nGreg held out his hand for it first. Maui stared at him in betrayal. They argued cheerfully over the roll until I tore it in half, which struck me as considerably better music than a speech about sharing.\n\nAround midday Steven arrived carrying a small tin of snacks. He waved to me, hugged Greg, and asked whether he was interrupting.\n\n\"Not at all,\" Greg said. \"We're doing complicated artistic work.\"\n\nSteven looked at the three of us sitting silently.\n\n\"Looks intense.\"\n\n\"Very,\" Maui agreed.\n\nSteven laughed and settled at the edge of the bench. He didn't ask to hear a private song before it was ready. Greg played a bit of the new melody because he wanted to, not because his son had to be an audience.\n\nBy late afternoon the song still had only two lines.\n\nGreg looked happier.\n\nOn the walk back to Tideglass, Maui carried the empty basket. He had a strange expression, as though he'd done a difficult task and wasn't sure whether anyone had noticed.\n\n\"Did you enjoy today?\" I asked.\n\n\"I was waiting for Greg to need me.\"\n\n\"He did.\"\n\n\"I didn't fix anything.\"\n\n\"You listened.\"\n\n\"Very heroic.\"\n\n\"Not everything is supposed to be.\"\n\nHe stopped beside the sea-glass garden. The raised beds were full of the tiny, surviving signs of the season. A white flower bent in the salt wind but did not break.\n\n\"Sometimes I worry,\" he said, \"that if I'm not doing something remarkable, you won't need me.\"\n\nI took his hand. \"I don't want you because you're necessary. I want you because I like the person who sits beside me, even when there's nothing to rescue.\"\n\nHe looked down at me.\n\n\"That person says very few intelligent things.\"\n\n\"True.\"\n\n\"Terrible cook.\"\n\n\"Frequently.\"\n\n\"Excellent at carrying blankets.\"\n\n\"Legendary.\"\n\nHe laughed and drew me close enough to kiss. The kiss was gentle, a little shy, warmed by the long ordinary day.\n\nLater Greg sent a note. He had kept the space between the notes, written one new line, and left the song unfinished.\n\nBeneath it he added: *Tell Maui thanks for sitting with me.*\n\nI showed Maui.\n\nHe read it twice.\n\nThen he put the note beside the tiny wooden fish, where he kept things that made him remember the difference between being admired and being wanted.\n\nThat night he didn't tell me a heroic story.\n\nWe listened to the sea until we were ready to go inside.","word_count":2026},"maui-47":{"number":47,"title":"Night at the Reopened Channel","subtitle":"Lanternweald — sixth expansion pass","content":"The rain arrived just after we finished fastening the last lantern hook.\n\nAt first it was only a mist, the kind that blurred the sea-glass orchard into soft green shapes and made every stone look freshly washed. Then the wind shifted, and real rain came across Tideglass in silver lines.\n\nFen looked at the gauges.\n\n\"Well,\" they said, \"here's our test.\"\n\nThe temporary surface channel had been cleaned, braced, and reopened after its controlled-flow trials. It carried water along an accessible maintenance route toward the shore, away from the gardens and lower homes. The older underground passage remained sealed. Nobody had pretended that building one good channel meant we could ignore the other forever.\n\nTonight's storm would tell us whether the surface repairs could hold through actual weather.\n\nTamsin stood by the upstream marker with a waterproof notebook. Danse monitored the portable pump. Sela watched the garden beds from beneath a canvas awning, refusing to leave until she knew her crops weren't about to drown again. Edda and Pip were checking the safe path, making sure the lanterns stayed visible through the rain.\n\nMaui carried a coil of spare rope.\n\nI carried hot tea.\n\nIt wasn't a glamorous division of labor.\n\nIt was ours.\n\nThe first hour passed slowly. Water rose against the channel's inner wall, then moved through the new outlet. The gauges stayed within the posted range. The lower service passage did not ring; Fen marked the absence of a sound as carefully as they would have marked its return.\n\nA burst of rain came harder.\n\nThe upstream reading climbed.\n\n\"Hold the flow,\" Fen called.\n\nDanse adjusted the pump. Tamsin checked the joint where the previous test had shown a leak. Sela leaned forward, watching the shallow runnel beside her garden.\n\nFor a moment the water edged toward the soil.\n\nMy heart lurched.\n\nThen the channel took the load and carried it away.\n\nSela released a breath she'd been holding for far too long.\n\n\"Good,\" she whispered.\n\nMaui looked at me.\n\nI could see the impulse to cheer, to lift someone into the air, to turn the moment into a victory for a hero. He stayed where he was and handed Fen the next measurement sheet.\n\nBy midnight the rain had eased.\n\nThe channel held through the tested conditions. The engineers would still need to inspect it in daylight, and the lower passage remained closed pending another round of structural work. The result was good, but it was not a guarantee for every storm the valley would ever see.\n\nWe recorded it honestly.\n\nSela arrived with the garden's rain record tucked beneath her coat. The lower beds had stayed dry, but one row of young plants was flattened where someone had walked across the soft soil.\n\n\"Who was out there?\" she asked.\n\nMaui opened his mouth. I could see the apology forming before he had checked whether it was his fault.\n\nFen turned the page. \"The inspection team used the marked route. This looks like the old animal path.\"\n\n\"Then don't let anybody guess whose footprints those are,\" Sela said.\n\nI wrote the damage down separately from the channel report. We would need to replace the plants, but it mattered that a working drain had not been turned into a false accusation.\n\nMaui rubbed the back of his neck. \"I could grow them again. Or lift the whole garden onto a cliff. Very scenic.\"\n\n\"No cliffs,\" Sela answered.\n\n\"Just the plants, then?\"\n\n\"Ask me after the soil dries.\"\n\nHe grinned despite himself. \"A firm maybe. I respect it.\"\n\nLater we passed the public board, where Pip had pinned an ordinary notice explaining that the channel had held for one tested storm, not every future storm. Somebody had drawn a tiny fish beside the measurement date. Mini Maui puffed his chest, insulted that the fish was getting more attention than he was.\n\n\"You could sign it,\" I told him.\n\n\"And spoil the mystery? Never.\"\n\nThen he put his arm around me and asked whether we could go somewhere quiet before the next repair call. I told him yes, if he promised not to invent a heroic title for taking a break.\n\n\"Too late,\" he said. \"The Great Rest of Maui has begun.\"\n\nI kissed his cheek. He smelled of rain, salt and the bread we'd carried through the storm. Our doors were still open to other loves and friendships; that made this particular walk home a choice, not a claim.\n\nThen we went home.\n\nMaui took off his wet cloak by the door. Water ran from the hem onto the floor. He looked at the puddle, then at me.\n\nMini Maui appeared at the edge of his shoulder and pantomimed a magnificent victory parade through our tiny kitchen.\n\n\"No,\" I said, before the real Maui had opened his mouth.\n\n\"How do you keep doing that?\"\n\n\"Your tattoos are terrible at keeping secrets.\"\n\nHe looked down. \"Traitor.\"\n\nThen he shook the cloak, sending water over both our feet, and bowed as if he'd just saved an entire fleet.\n\n\"Behold the mighty Maui, conqueror of the storm! Successfully carried rope, followed the map, and didn't collapse a single historic building. You're welcome.\"\n\n\"You're dripping on the clean floor.\"\n\n\"An outrageous criticism of a flawless hero.\"\n\nHis grin was bright enough to make me laugh, but when he looked toward the window I could see the worry he'd been hiding. The channel had held for tonight's conditions. Tomorrow might be different. He wanted an ending with a great speech. We had a wet kitchen and another inspection scheduled.\n\nHe fetched a cloth and wiped the floor while humming a terrible victory tune. It sounded suspiciously like a song he intended to perform to an audience of two.\n\n\"I could make a speech about what we survived,\" he said.\n\n\"Please don't.\"\n\n\"Not even a short one?\"\n\n\"You're dripping on the clean boards.\"\n\nHe laughed and fetched a cloth.\n\nBefore we went inside, Fen called us back to the tideboard. The gauge was below its warning line, but the rain had left a pile of leaves against the screen. The system had worked within the conditions posted for it; it wasn't a promise that tomorrow's debris would clear itself.\n\n\"We'll need a morning shift to inspect the grate,\" Fen said. \"No one goes down the historic barrier. That stays closed.\"\n\nMaui looked at the water, then at the coil of rope he'd carried all evening.\n\n\"I could stay and guard it.\"\n\n\"You could,\" Fen answered. \"Or you could let the paid watch do the job they signed up for.\"\n\nHe looked faintly wounded. \"Am I being dismissed?\"\n\n\"You're being thanked. Go home.\"\n\nTamsin sent the initial numbers to Neri for the public board, carefully separating observed flow from the forecast. Sela wanted to know whether the shallowest bed had survived. Fen told her what the gauge could establish and what she'd need to check in daylight. Nobody offered her a promise based on darkness and optimism.\n\nEdda came down the upper path after confirming the shelter door was visible and unlocked. She was tired enough to stop beside us and rub her wrists.\n\n\"Please don't ask me to plan another grand opening,\" she said. \"I have had enough ceremonies for a season.\"\n\n\"Not even a small one?\" Maui asked.\n\n\"Especially not a small one. Somebody always asks me to move the chairs.\"\n\nI laughed. We helped return the lanterns to their hooks, then signed out of the shift so Neri would know who remained on duty. The little act made the night feel less like an adventure and more like a place where people had learned to care for their own.\n\nAs we walked home, Maui reached for my hand, then withdrew it.\n\n\"Sorry. Your fingers are cold.\"\n\n\"They're cold because I was holding a mug in the rain. You can still ask.\"\n\nHe smiled. \"May I?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nHe took my hand between both of his, warming it as we passed the garden. For a moment I wanted to tell him that nothing else in the world mattered. That would have been romantic, and untrue. Sela's seedlings mattered. The paid watch mattered. Our friends and other partners mattered. I was glad there was room for all of it, even on a night when I wanted to go home with him more than anything.\n\nThe house lights came into view. He lifted our joined hands and kissed my knuckles, then opened the door for me. There was wet flooring to mop and tea to make, and for once we were both delighted to be ordinary.\n\nI lit the kitchen stove while he dried the floor. The house smelled of damp wool, warm wood, and the rosemary we'd hung to dry beside the window. Outside, the sea still moved against the stones, but the rain had softened to a steady whisper.\n\nWe made tea.\n\nNeither of us wanted dinner.\n\nFor a while we sat without speaking.\n\nMaui looked toward the window.\n\n\"Look at that,\" Maui said, gesturing toward our reflections in the rainy window. \"Two magnificent heroes. One with much better hair.\" His grin stayed in place for a moment, then slipped. \"Are you happy?\"\n\nI considered.\n\n\"I'm relieved. Tired. A little frightened about the next storm. And yes, I'm happy.\"\n\nHe smiled.\n\n\"I was thinking about the first time we stood by that landing.\"\n\n\"When you promised to fix everything?\"\n\n\"I was magnificent.\"\n\n\"You were unbearable.\"\n\n\"Both.\"\n\nI laughed.\n\nHe took my hand across the table.\n\n\"I thought if I could keep you safe, you'd never need to leave.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"And now?\"\n\n\"Now I know you have to be able to leave. Otherwise staying doesn't mean what I want it to mean.\"\n\nI felt my throat tighten.\n\n\"That's the thing I've been trying to learn too.\"\n\nHe moved closer.\n\n\"Can I ask for something?\"\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\n\"Stay with me tonight. Not because the storm was frightening, or because the village needs us, or because I earned it. Because you want to.\"\n\nI looked at him.\n\n\"I do.\"\n\nHe leaned toward me slowly, giving me time to meet him. I kissed him, tasting the tea and the faint salt of the air. His fingers brushed my cheek, then settled against my waist when I drew him closer.\n\nFor a moment I forgot the soaked cloak, the gauges, the repairs. There was only the warmth of him, the small crackle of the fire, and the way his breath caught when I kissed him again.\n\n\"Are you cold?\" he whispered.\n\n\"A little.\"\n\n\"Would you like the blanket?\"\n\n\"Eventually.\"\n\nHe smiled against my mouth.\n\nI laughed.\n\nWe turned down the lamps and left the dishes for morning. The rest of the night belonged to us, quiet and tender behind a closed door, with no need to describe it to anyone else.\n\nWhen I woke, the rain had stopped.\n\nMaui was sitting on the edge of the bed, trying to untangle a knot in his hair with the kind of concentration he usually reserved for storms.\n\n\"Need help?\" I asked.\n\n\"Absolutely not.\"\n\nI watched him struggle for another minute.\n\n\"Would you like help?\" I asked again.\n\nHe sighed.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nI sat beside him, worked the knot free, and kissed his shoulder.\n\nOutside, Fen's dawn bell sounded once.\n\nThe old channel did not answer.\n\nBefore breakfast Fen stopped at the door carrying a damp notebook and the narrow pole used to measure debris at the upper grate. Their coat was still dripping from the night shift.\n\n“Don't celebrate yet,” they told Maui. “We need the first report written before anyone decides we can leave the barriers open indefinitely.”\n\nMaui had been halfway through lifting the breakfast table to move it out of their way. He set it down so gently that not even the cups rattled.\n\n“Aw, come on. The channel held!”\n\n“It did,” Fen said. “At the tide level we measured. Not every tide forever.”\n\nI took the notebook. The night crew had recorded three readings, one blocked branch and a short stretch of accessible path that would need new grip stones before the next storm. The lower historic passage remained sealed, and no one had used it to reach the inspection point.\n\n“Can we help with the path?” I asked.\n\n“Later, after the engineers approve the materials. Not by rearranging the shore before breakfast.”\n\nMaui pulled a face. Mini Maui threw up its tiny hands in outrage.\n\n“We are being persecuted by paperwork,” Maui announced.\n\n“By measuring things,” Fen corrected.\n\n“Even worse.”\n\nI laughed and offered Fen a dry cloth for the notebook. They accepted, then asked if the morning report could state the tide limit in plain language. Not every traveler would know what the marks on the gauge meant. The public notice needed to say when the upper route closed and whom to contact if the grate filled again.\n\nMaui found a pencil and added the word *ask* to the bottom of the draft. It was crooked. He looked very pleased with it.\n\nAt breakfast we received the inspection message: the temporary surface system had passed its first real storm test. Another inspection was scheduled, and the lower passage would remain sealed.\n\nMaui read the note aloud, then set it beside the calendar.\n\n\"Good enough for today,\" he said.\n\nI smiled.\n\nWe ate bread, cleaned the wet floor, and went to help Sela check the garden.\n\nNothing was finished forever.\n\nBut something important had held.","word_count":2240}};var s=lib.series.find(x=>x.series_slug==="the-shore-i-choose");if(!s)return;for(var i=0;i<s.chapters.length;i++){var c=s.chapters[i],e=edits[c.id];if(e)Object.assign(c,e);}s.total_word_count=90385;})();
